#518 lcabadval -- TABLE 2 PREDICTIONS, written 2026-09-10 ~00:40 BEFORE any mutant was built.
Its own mtime is the proof of order; every <mutant>.raw in this directory is younger.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  A prediction written after the measurement is not a prediction, and the
carrier's frozen-number class is exactly a number that arrived before the run that produced it.
Inverting the discipline makes the predictions USEFUL rather than dangerous: they are recorded
BEFORE, so a KILL that refutes one is as informative as a sweep that confirms one, and TABLE2.out
can state which cells were reasoned correctly and which were not.  Nothing here is a measurement.

DERIVED FROM: the frozen 58-row instrument (lca_badval_probe.REPAIRED58.asrun.py) read
session-by-session, and the frozen baseline t2/BASELINE_t2.c read arm-by-arm.  No build was run.

A NOTE ON SESSION DEATH.  Every DETECTOR row is written `X if alive else "dead"', so ONE host
kill reds EVERY row of that session.  In the witness block only `<arm> legal' reds on a death:
`<arm> live' prints values captured before it and `<arm> attrib' is green in both directions.
That is why the (b) reinstatements score so much lower than the (d) edits -- it is a property of
the instrument's shape, not a measure of how bad the defect is.

mutant                  pred    predicted reddening rows
