## Q1 - premise audit

**(a) READ:** the field is `f = (idata & 0xfff00000) >> 20`, so the domain is the 4096 values 0x000-0xfff; `f+1` cannot overflow (max 0x1000 in 64-bit). **REASONED, enumerated:** for any f, let t be its lowest zero bit; f+1 clears bits 0..t-1 and sets bit t, so `f & (f+1)` equals f's bits *above* t. That is zero iff f has no set bit above its lowest zero - i.e. f = 2^k - 1. Walking k = 0..12: **0x000, 0x001, 0x003, 0x007, 0x00f, 0x01f, 0x03f, 0x07f, 0x0ff, 0x1ff, 0x3ff, 0x7ff, 0xfff** - thirteen values; every other f fails (spot-walk: f=5, 5&6=4?0; f=6, 6&7=6?0; f=7, 7&8=0 �). This matches S2's Table 6-6 characterization *as the packet states it*; the manual rows themselves I cannot read - settling fact: the thirteen data rows at :9586-9598. Cross-check (READ): every detector value classifies correctly - 0x100000, 0x700000 (f=7), 0x3ff00000 (f=0x3ff), 0xfff00000 accepted; 0x500000 (f=0b1010000), 0x900000 (f=9) declined.

**(b) REASONED: no.** The old guard `idata & ~1` (READ, inlined) accepted only {0,1}, so a live old guest only ever had `ioc_conf` ? {0,1}. The new store `& 0x3` preserves bit 0 for every input; bit 1 becomes visible only on writes the old code killed the host for. **Not settleable:** that `lca_pci_conf` reads bit 0 only - asserted in a diff comment, not inlined. Settling fact: the one line of `lca_pci_conf` that tests `d->ioc_conf`.

**(c) REASONED: yes, under the packet's own layout.** 0x240000000 = 233 + 23�: bit 33 set, bit 32 clear; Figure 6-9 as inlined (S2) puts WEN at ?33?, SG at ?32? - so WEN-set, base field 0x400. Likewise 0x280000000 = 233 + 231 (D1's `NORMAL["wbase0"]`): WEN-set, base 0x800. So `wbase1 wen` and `wen once` test what they claim. **Not settleable:** the numeric values of `IOC_W_BASE_WEN/SG/W_BASE` in alpha_lcareg.h - if WEN were ?32?, `wen once` would score 0 and self-redden, but the claim rests on the un-inlined defines.

**(d) NOT CHECKABLE, both halves.** The packet asserts 0x1e0 is inside DEVINIT's window and absent from alpha_lcareg.h but inlines neither the window extent nor the header's offset list. Settling facts: the lca_ioc DEVINIT length (is it > 0x1e0?) and the case/offset list. Note D5 partially self-checks - if the offset fell outside, the announce rows would score 0 and redden - but that is a run-time fact, not a packet fact.

**(e) REASONED: no regression; one new diagnostic.** Enumerating the old accepted sets (READ, re-derived from the inlined old guards; all match S1): CONF {0,1}  stored identically, no announce; TB_ENA {0,0x80}  identical; W_BASE0 {0, 0x300800000}  masked store = 0x300800000, identical; W_MASK0 {0x700000}  f=7 valid, identical; W_BASE1 {0x240000000}  identical; W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}  f=0x3ff valid, identical. Read arms were never fatal and are untouched. The default arm's old accepted set was empty. The only behaviour change on an old-accepted value: 0x300800000 and 0x240000000 carry WEN, so each now prints one "a DMA window was enabled" line where the old code was silent - host-console output, no guest-visible state change.

## Q2 - smallest edit that passes all 40

**REASONED: in a W_MASK arm, change `f = v >> 20` to `f = idata >> 20`** - one identifier, keeps the store mask. Effect: any W_MASK write with a bit above 31 set - manual-legal (type X ignored), and producer-emittable, since quadword writes carry 64 bits and the old guards compared full 64-bit values - gets f � 0x1000, fails the rule, is **declined**. That is #518's own defect class, silently reinstated. All rows stay green: every inlined W_MASK value (D2's 0x100000/0x500000/0x900000/0xfff00000; accepted 0x700000/0x3ff00000) is field-only, so extracting from idata vs. v coincides. The row that looks like it should catch this and stays green: **`wmask0 declined`** (and `wmask0 stored`). This is the cell 5 lacks: its rule axis is {kept/weakened/strengthened} over the *comparison*; the extraction *domain* is not a cell. **Contingency:** D1's legal values come from `ARMS`, which is not inlined - if any witness row writes W_MASK above bit 31, this reddens there. Settling fact: the ARMS wmask tuples. (A fully settleable runner-up: Q4's latch removals.)

## Q3 - option-set completeness

The grep I cannot do - dev_gt.c and dev_footbridge.c are named, not inlined; the settling fact is those arms' disposition for unknown encodings. From the packet, unnamed members: **(i)** sub-quadword access width - the producer can emit 1/2/4-byte writes; "quadword only" constrains the guest, not what the emulator receives; old code died on most such writes (a byte 0x01 to W_MASK0), new code field-extracts silently; every row is quadword (disclosed) and no sentence names the width member. **(ii)** W_MASK field 0x000 - inside the thirteen (READ, S2), accepted by the fix, but no inlined row writes it; a mutant `(f & (f+1)) == 0 && f != 0` passes every inlined row; whether a witness row pins it is not settleable (witness values not inlined). **(iii)** The reset values of the six registers - no row reads before writing; no design sentence states them.

## Q4 - the floor

**Not "none".** Within the author's own axes, the {latch removed} cell is empty at two of the six latch bits (denominator: 6 bits x {kept/removed/shared} = 18): **WMASK1** - D2 gives MASK1 exactly one declined write, so `wmask1 indep` and `wmask1 declined` are fixed under latch-removal; **DEF_R** - D5 does two writes but one read, so `def r once` (which kills *sharing*, not removal) is fixed. Both are one-edit mutants that break the bounded-announce contract. Additionally, "shared" is rowed only within the pairs the sessions mix; a cross-class collision (e.g., MASK0's decline latching on the WEN bit) has no row because no session mixes classes - 3's sentence that the indep rows "pin" collisions overclaims. Outside the floor entirely: the mask axis has no *widened* member - CONF `& 0x7`, TB_ENA `& 0xc0`, a W_BASE mask including bit 0, W_MASK `& 0x3fff00000` all pass, because no row writes the reserved bits they would newly store.

## Q5 - accept-row failability

The live rows' channel is alive-vs-dead; a **decline-style** narrowing cannot redden them - only re-escalation (the (b) mutants M1-M7) can. The author's M23 as worded - "W_BASE0 rejecting 0x300000000" - **reddens nothing**: no accept row writes 0x300000000 (the live value normalizes to 0x280000000 per `NORMAL`, and on HEAD it must lie outside the old whitelist or it would not have been RED), and `wbase0 stored` is invariant to declining the accepted write (the legal write follows and overwrites). The parenthetical is inverted: the old whitelist re-added *re-accepts* 0x300000000 and rejects 0x280000000 - as a decline it reddens `wbase0 stored`, not `wbase0 live`; as the old fatal+exit it is M3. `wbase0 zero`: failable � (M_base0_nonzero leaves 0x280000000 from D4 phase 0, ? 0x0). `wbase1 wen`: failable � (WEN dropped from the mask reads 0x40000000).

## Q6 - claim  row gaps

Two added sentences assert observables with no row and no 2 entry: **(1)** "(it cannot be silenced by -q.)" - no row runs -q; **(2)** "Bit 0 is what lca_pci_conf reads" - no row triggers a config cycle with CFG1 set; its companion "type-1 . not modelled" is likewise untested (the announce's *presence* is rowed; the truth of "not modelled" is not).

## Else found; read vs. took

- **READ:** the diff, the detector block, the pretable, S1-S6. **Took on faith:** the manual's transcribed contents, S3's output files, and every un-inlined file. S3's scores are the packet's claims; I cannot measure.
