**Q2. Smallest edit that breaks and passes all 40 rows - REASONED.**

Edit: `d->ioc_conf = idata & 0x3ULL;`  `d->ioc_conf = idata & 0x7ULL;` (one character).
Why it breaks: HRM S6.2.9 defines IOC_CONF<1:0> as a 2-bit field; bit 2 is reserved. The fix's stated contract is "store the ARCHITECTED FIELDS, ignore reserved bits." Storing bit 2 violates that contract.
Why it passes: every CONF test value in the detector is 0x100000002 (bit 32 set, bits 0-31 zero). `0x100000002 & 0x7 = 0x2`; `0x100000002 & 0x3 = 0x2`. The `conf stored` row reads back 0x2 in both cases. `conf reserved` reads 0x2. `conf once` counts "IOC_CONF<1> set" - the bit-1 condition is unchanged, count stays 1. No witness row exercises bit 2 either (the accepted set is {0,1}).
The row that stays green: `conf stored` (and, by the same arithmetic, every other row).
The author's PRETABLE 5 enumeration lists `{mask kept / narrowed / dropped}` - **widened is absent**, and this is the cell with no row. (Widening to `& 0xFFFFFFFF` *is* caught - `conf reserved` would read 0x100000002, expect 0x2. The gap is specifically for widening that doesn't reach the highest reserved bit tested.)

