---

**Q1(a) - the field rule. REASONED (enumerated).**
`(f & (f+1)) == 0` accepts exactly f ? {0,1,3,7,15,31,63,127,255,511,1023,2047,4095}. Check: if f = 2^k-1 then f+1 = 2^k and f & 2^k = 0; conversely f & (f+1) clears f's trailing ones and keeps the rest, so a zero result forces f to be all trailing ones, i.e. 2^k-1. Over 12 bits k = 0..12  exactly 13 values; k=13 (8191) is out of range. f = 0xfff corner: f+1 = 0x1000 computed in uint64, no wrap, passes. So the predicate is **exactly** the thirteen contiguous-low-ones encodings - *conditional on S2's claim that Table 6-6's thirteen values are those* (manual not in packet; taken as stated).
Caveat the brief did not state: the extraction `v = idata & 0xfff00000` silently drops idata bits <63:32> and <19:0>, so the accepted *input* class is larger than thirteen values - correctness of that depends on the RES type of W_MASK's non-field bits. Not settleable from packet. Single fact that settles it: the HRM's type for W_MASK bits outside <31:20> (if type X, the normalization is right).

**Q1(b) - IOC_CONF & 0x3 vs lca_pci_conf. REASONED.** (idata & 0x3) & 1 � idata & 1, so bit 0 - the claimed consumer - is identical for every input. For the old accepted set {0,1} (S1, verified against the old predicate `idata & ~1`) the stored value is unchanged. Whether *any other* site reads d->ioc_conf above bit 0: not settleable from packet (the consumer list of `d->ioc_conf` is the named fact), but moot for regressions since old code killed the process on any value � 2.

**Q1(c) - 0x240000000 WEN-set. REASONED (enumerated).** 0x240000000 = 0x200000000 | 0x40000000 = bits {33, 30}. Bit 33 = WEN per S2/Figure 6-9 �; bit 30 ? <31:20> �, so it round-trips the store mask (0x3fff00000) intact  `wbase1 wen` want of 0x240000000 is consistent. `wen once`'s other write, 0x280000000 = bits {33, 31}, also has WEN �, so D4 really does issue two WEN writes. One dependency: the numeric values of IOC_W_BASE_W_BASE/SG/WEN are not inlined; bit *positions* are (S2), and the detector's reported round-trips are internally consistent with mask = 0x3fff00000.

**Q1(d) - +0x1e0 and the default arm. REASONED from packet-internal evidence; raw fact not checkable.** I cannot see DEVINIT's window bounds or alpha_lcareg.h. But the D5 needles embed the offset: "unimplemented write to offset 0x1e0" is printed by the default arm with `(int)relative_addr`. The reported 40/40 on the fix (READ: S3, pre-table) therefore means the default arm *was* reached at relative_addr 0x1e0 - i.e. 0x1e0 is mapped to this device and claimed by no case. If run outputs are trusted as stated, (d) holds; the underlying file facts (window length, register list) are the named items.

**Q1(e) - accepted-value regressions. REASONED (enumerated over all seven old-accepted writes):**
- conf 00, 11 � silent; tbena 00, 0x800x80 (0x80 & 0x80) � silent
- wmask0 0x700000 (f=7 �) stored unchanged, silent; wmask1 0x3ff00000 (f=0x3ff �) unchanged, silent
- wbase0 0x300800000 = bits {23,32,33} ? mask  stored identical - **but WEN (33) is set: one new `(once)` announce line now prints**
- wbase1 0x240000000 = bits {30,33}  stored identical, same one-time WEN announce (shared latch, so one line total per instance)
State transitions on all accepted values are bit-identical; read-backs identical; host alive as before. The **only** observable delta on a previously accepted value is the single WEN announce. That is disclosed design ("announced once when any window is ENABLED"), not a regression - window translation was unmodelled before and after. No silent behavior change found.

---

**Q2 - smallest breaking edit that passes all 40.**
**M20_def_echo itself** - e.g. one line in the default arm making a read return the written/last value. Every row stays green, specifically **`def read0` stays green**, and here is the mechanism (REASONED, walking `seq`): `seq` emits `step n; print t3` after *every* phase. D5's phases are `[[st, st], [ld]]` - phase 0 contains **no load**, so `vals[0]` is the print of t3 in its reset/stale state (0x0 on the fix build - the reported 40/40 proves the stale value matches want "0x0"), and `vals[1]`, the actual read-back, **is consumed by no row**. `def read0` reads `nth(vals, 0)`. This is precisely the vacuity PRETABLE 4 claims to have excluded ("no row's channel is fixed under its named mutant") - they caught the same shape for `wbase1 stored` and missed it here. Consequence: Table 1's M20 cell will record a spurious "survived". One-line repair: `nth(vals, 1)`, or give D5 phase 0 a trailing `ld`. Single measurement I'd want but cannot take: run M20_def_echo against the probe and watch `def read0` stay green (or just dump `vals` for the D5 session).
Three further one-edit all-green mutants (cells the enumeration does not contain): **remove the DEF_R latch guard** (D5 performs only ONE read, so removing the read latch still announces exactly once - `def r once` pins *shared*, not *removed*); **drop IOC_W_BASE_SG from both W_BASE masks** (every read-back constant in the detector - 0x280000000, 0x240000000, 0x0 - has bit 32 clear; the only SG-carrying write, accepted-first 0x300800000, is overwritten before any read); **widen the conf mask** (e.g. `& 0xffffffff`: the only out-of-field bit any conf row writes is bit 32 of 0x100000002, which any �32-bit mask drops anyway  all conf rows green - "widened" sits between their {narrowed, dropped} cells and is unnamed).

**Q3 - option-set completeness.**
Cannot grep - the fork's idiom census is not settleable from the packet, and I say so rather than reconcile. From the packet's own material (READ): the same switch two cases above the default arm already contains the **store-and-ignore** member (W_T_BASE0/1 store raw, nothing consumes them - S5/PRETABLE Asymmetry 1), and pass 1 named dev_gt.c's stored-but-unused registers as precedent. So at the default arm the option set the design named is {decline write / zero read / once-announce}; the member it did **not** name, despite in-file precedent, is **store-but-don't-use** (decline functionally, echo on read). That member may be wrong for reserved offsets - but the packet offers no principled line for why "unmodelled" means *store raw* at T_BASE and *store nothing* two cases later; PRETABLE itself only owes a sentence for T_BASE. At W_MASK the unnamed members are: store-the-invalid-encoding-anyway (dev_gt style) and zero-on-invalid; "keep previous" is a modeling choice - the HRM fact that would settle it (behavior on a write of a non-Table-6-6 encoding) is not in the packet. Also unnamed at the default arm: read-returns-all-ones (some buses answer unmapped with ~0); real-LCA behavior on reserved offsets is not in the packet.

**Q4 - the floor: cells with no channel-varying row. REASONED.**
Not "none". In the author's framing (6 arms x {kept/narrowed/dropped} x {store/no-store} = 36; W_MASK rule {kept/weak/strong} x {order} for two arms � 12; latches {kept/removed/shared} x 6 bits = 18; ~66 cells), the cells with no varying row:
1. **default-arm read-value {echo vs zero}** - row exists, channel dead (Q2 index bug).
2. **def-read latch {removed}** - D5 reads once (above); only *shared* is rowed.
3. **W_BASE mask {SG-dropped}** - no read-back constant exercises bit 32 (above). M11 pins WEN, M12 pins truncation to 32 bits; the SG-only drop is a distinct unnamed cell.
4. **conf mask {widened, bits 2-31 kept}** - no conf write carries bits 31:2 (above); unnamed by the {kept/narrowed/dropped} trio.
5. **cross-arm latch sharing** (confwen, maskwen, defwen, .): `wmask1 indep` and `def r once` arm a sibling's bit first *within* a pair; no session arms a third party's bit before the firing write, and D3/D4/D5 are separate sessions. A one-#define collision (e.g. CFG1 == WEN bit) survives.
6. **tbena {no-store}** - cannot be settled from the packet: every D1 session writes the old-accepted value first, and for tbena NORMAL == 0x80 equals one of the two old-accepted values {0, 0x80} (S1). If the probe's `ARMS` constant for tbena is 0x80, a store-skipping mutant reads back 0x80 and the row is vacuous. The probe's per-arm `accepted` constants are not inlined - naming that literal as the settling fact. (All other arms are safe: conf {0,1} vs 0x2 �, base0 {0,0x300800000} vs 0x280000000 �, base1 0x240000000 vs 0x0 � - their own note - mask0 0x700000 vs 0x100000 �, mask1 0x3ff00000 vs 0xfff00000 �.)
7. Granularity caveat, cell rowed but thin: the W_MASK rows exercise fields {1, 0xfff} accept and {5, 9} decline only - 11 of the 13 legal encodings never round-trip, so any strengthened rule that keeps {1, 0xfff} and rejects 5 and 9 (e.g. a restored mini-whitelist of the original bug's shape) survives all 40 rows. M13/M14 represent the cell; the cell is rowed, the enumeration isn't.

**Q5 - accept-row failability. REASONED.**
`live` rows have channel alive/dead, so their growth mutant must **reintroduce death**, not merely decline (decline-style narrowing is the `stored` rows' job, and is pinned there except as noted in Q4.6): conf-legal  {0,1} whitelist with fatal under the new mask; tbena-legal  fatal on `idata & ~TEN`; wmask0-legal  `!= 0x700000` fatal; wmask1-legal  `!= 0x3ff00000` fatal; wbase0-legal  author's M23 (`!= 0 && != 0x300800000` fatal under the mask); wbase1-legal  `!= 0x240000000` fatal, which also kills the SROM zero. For the two named extras: `wbase0 zero` - correctly NOT failable by (b)/M23 (base0's old whitelist accepted zero; the packet says so and I verified the predicate), its mutant is decline-on-zero at base0, and D4's phase-0 write of 0x280000000 before the zero makes the row non-vacuous (declined zero would read back 0x280000000) �; `wbase1 wen` - failable by WEN-dropped mask (reads 0x40000000) or by decline-WEN-values (reads 0). Both have growth mutants; no accept row is green-by-construction.

**Q6 - claim  row: two unrowed, unsentenced comment claims. READ (diff) + REASONED.**
1. Helper comment: "fatal() prints and returns (it cannot be silenced by -q...)". 2 rows "prints and returns" but the **-q clause** is an observable host-behavior claim with no row (no -q session exists in the probe) and no sentence in 2.
