tools/pipeline/ledger.json-24893-   "hold_question": "TEX is ACCEPT only in the MIRRORED form -- same `>> 3` as the store -- which round-trips per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.  Is shipping a knowingly-wrong-but-self-consistent readback better than DECLINING that one arm until sgitexindex lands?  And is the round-trip row genuinely sufficient coupling enforcement, or does a mirrored TEX read make the wrong index HARDER to fix by giving it a green row?",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24894:   "state_note": "OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED\nHERE.  ALL THREE WERE. ***\n\n**1. THE CLASS COUNT IS WRONG IN THE HEADLINE, FOR THE FOURTH TIME ON THIS ROW.**  `grep -cE\n'^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` gives 12 STATEMENTS, but `:1537` **cannot run**: it is the trailing `else`\nof `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal; exit(1); }` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)` --\nan exhaustive pair with a third arm.  Already filed as [[sgimtewidth]].\n\n> **ELEVEN guest-reachable kills, in two classes: 6 DIRECTION + 5 MTE-feature.**  This row has now\n> carried 21 -> 19 -> 12 -> 11, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*\n\n*** PASS 2: SIX SEATS, ALL SIX ANSWERED, THREE SAID DO NOT SHIP -- AND TWO OF THE THREE WERE\nRIGHT. ***\n\n    codex     DO NOT SHIP   an L2 FLOOR BREACH, with a predicted score and its mechanism\n    grok      DO NOT SHIP   records; and it re-measured every claim against the tree\n    glm       DO NOT SHIP   FALSE ALARM -- but it named the settling fact instead of guessing\n    kimi      SHIP, cond.   the enum record -- a real condition, now met\n    deepseek  SHIP\n    minimax   SHIP\n\n## THE L2 -- a clause-(d) floor breach, repaired in-round\n\n`tex xbit` fires the six WIDTH latches only, so it sweeps bits 0-5.  codex named the escape:\n\n    SGIRE_W_TEX_RD = 1 << 11  ->  1 << 8  (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)      PREDICTED 44/44\n\nand gave the reason from the detector's own session schedule -- **the four TEX-read scenarios and\nthe unmapped-read scenario never share an emulator instance**, so bit 8 is clear whenever TEX is\nread.  *\"M10 establishes detection of a width-bit collision, not every existing-bit collision\"* --\nwhich is exactly the over-generalisation my CHANNEL table had made in a parenthesis.\n\n**Repaired with two SAME-INSTANCE rows, `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, in BOTH orderings** (one\nordering would leave the mirror open -- the `#499` shape).  Gate raised 44 -> 46 in the same edit.\n\n## THREE WRONG RECORDS, ALL MINE, ALL CORRECTED IN PLACE\n\n* **the enum comment** -- *\"The next free bit is 1 << 11; the enum ends HERE on purpose\"* went false\n  twice over when this diff took `1 << 11`.  *** THE FILE DOCUMENTS THIS EXACT FAILURE TEN LINES\n  ABOVE WHERE I EDITED *** -- the identical claim on `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` went false when\n  `SGIRE_W_BADMODE` was added below it, and that comment records both the failure and the repair.\n  **I repeated a documented failure while editing the enum that documents it.**  Found by kimi,\n  confirmed independently by codex and grok.\n* **my LINEAR comment** claimed the `len == 8` guard \"keeps tlbi even\".  **Width does not imply\n  alignment** -- the guard tests LENGTH; CPU-side alignment is what keeps it even, and the OLDER\n  surrounding comment says so correctly.  My new wording was worse than the one it sat beside.\n* **the probe's contract header** still described six live read kills and row names this round\n  deleted -- clause (e), the thing a future reader trusts about what the detector claims.\n\n## *** A CLAIM OF MINE WAS CONFOUNDED, AND I HAD REPEATED IT ALL EVENING ***\n\nI said `M8_texaccept` measures the store-first TEX design.  codex: **M8 also removes the announce**,\nso its four failures do not isolate the store-first contribution -- both channels red under it.\nIsolating it needs a mutant that converts TEX to ACCEPT while KEEPING the announce.  *The\nstore-first design is still right for the reason grok gave at design time; what is not established\nis that M8 measured it.*\n\n## THE `odata` GAP -- three seats, independently\n\ncodex, glm and grok each found the same missing cell in my (d) enumeration.  **Coverage is\nunaffected** -- `M2_datafill` IS that mutant and it kills -- but the ENUMERATION could not have\ngenerated it.  Corrected in `PRETABLE.out` as an L4.\n\n## WHAT glm'S FALSE ALARM ACTUALLY FOUND -- a defect in MY BRIEF\n\nIt reasoned from HUNK BOUNDARIES that `check \"sgi_re controls live\"` sits in no hunk while the\ncontrol count went 2 -> 3, and enumerated two ways that could break the gate.  Reading the line\nsettles it: the check greps `OPUS497T_CONTROL=OK`, does not count controls, and the probe emits\nthat token over all three.  grok reached the same resolution independently and refused to ratify\nthe inference.\n\n> **A DIFF-ONLY PACKET OMITS UNCHANGED CONTEXT BY CONSTRUCTION, so a seat asking \"is the\n> surrounding code still consistent with this change?\" -- which is what pass 2 is FOR -- cannot\n> see it.**  That is the sketch rule one level up: the sketch rule says inline the enclosing\n> FUNCTION for a proposed edit; this says inline the enclosing BLOCK for a reviewed one.\n\n*Not proposed as a general rule on one instance: the seat named its settling fact, the cost was one\n`sed`, and a brief that inlines every adjacent block is a brief nobody reads.*\n\n*** THE L2 REPAIR IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED.  L2VERIFY.txt, scratch clone, frozen instrument. ***\n\n    BASE_pass2        46/46   the repaired detector, CLEAN\n    M13_latchcollide  44/46   KILLED   SGIRE_W_TEX_RD 1<<11 -> 1<<8 (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)\n\n**46 - 44 = exactly TWO red rows -- precisely `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, the two the repair\nadded, with nothing else perturbed.**  That is the cleanest possible signature for a targeted\nrepair: the new rows catch the named escape and no existing row's score moved.\n\n*** ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and it is stated rather than glossed: the panel PREDICTED 44/44\nagainst the PRE-REPAIR 44-row detector, and that prediction was NOT re-measured. ***  The 44-row\nform no longer exists once the two rows are added, so the escape itself is a PREDICTION with a\nmechanism (the four TEX-read scenarios and the unmapped-read scenario never share an instance;\n`tex xbit` sets bits 0-5 only), not a measured score.  **What is measured is that the repaired\ndetector kills it.**  Re-measuring the escape would mean rebuilding the 44-row detector to prove a\nhole I have already closed -- and the mechanism is checkable by reading the probe's session list,\nwhich is how the panel derived it.\n\nClone left `cmp`-CLEAN against SEC with no sentinel.\n\n*** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST EXPENSIVE: IT COST AN\nENTIRE EXTRA PASS.  Mine, on pass 2, found by grok on pass 3. ***\n\nPass 3 opened with four seats saying DO NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN, and grok's answer contained the line:\n\n> *\"The grok pass-2 seat already named bit 10 (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE`); that cell is still open.\"*\n\n**It had.**  grok's PASS-2 answer says, in full:\n\n> *\"`tex xbit` does `sw` at the six `SITES` bases, then one TEX `ld`.  That fires\n> `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` ... `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and nothing else.  **`SGIRE_W_BADMODE` (1<<10),\n> `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` (1<<8), `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` (1<<6), `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` (1<<9) are never set in\n> that session.**  `other decl` is a different process.  M10 only tested the WIDTH alias the row\n> actually populates.\"*\n\n**grok named FOUR bits on pass 2.  codex named ONE (bit 8).  I acted on codex's single cell,\nshipped a repair for it, and pass 3 then found the other three -- which one pass could have\nclosed.**\n\n*** AND I HAD THE FILE OPEN. ***  I read grok's pass-2 answer far enough to quote its resolution of\nglm's `controls live` objection -- which sits about twenty lines ABOVE the passage quoted here --\nand stopped at the premises table.  **This is not \"a seat answered and nobody looked\"; it is \"I\nlooked, took the part that confirmed my reading, and stopped before the part that would have cost\nme work.\"**  That is the harder version of the class and the one no checker can catch.\n\n**THE MEASURED COST:** one full extra pass -- a six-seat panel, a rebuild, a mutant table, and the\nclass-closure amendment invoked -- to reach a repair grok had specified a pass earlier.  It also\nmeans my pass-2 record said *\"repaired in-round\"* about a repair that closed one cell of five.\n\n> **THE HABIT THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT is the one this project already has and I did not apply:\n> READ THE BODY, NOT THE VERDICT LINE AND NOT THE FIRST SCREEN.**  The carrier states it for\n> verdict lines -- *\"a verdict line is written against the brief's framing and is the LEAST likely\n> part of an answer to hold the correction\"* -- and the same is true of an answer's opening\n> section, which addresses the brief's questions in the brief's order.  **grok's finding was in\n> its Q1 body, past the premises table.**\n\n*Recorded here rather than in a records row because the cost landed on THIS round, and because the\ninstance is specific: two seats answered the same question and I integrated the narrower answer\nwithout diffing it against the wider one.  When two seats answer one question, DIFF THEM.*\n\n*** A READING SEAT FOUND A LIVE BUG THAT A GREEN 46/46 RUN HAD JUST ENDORSED.  This is the most\nimportant thing in the round. ***\n\n`store()` is `store(A, addr, op=OP_SW_T0_A3, val=\"0x12345678\")`.  I wrote the six drawing-engine\nsetup calls as\n\n    cmds += store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B)\n\n**so MODE_LIN_B was assembled as an INSTRUCTION and the register received the default value.  All\nsix were wrong the same way.**  The row executed garbage opcodes and performed none of the register\nwrites its own comment described.\n\n*** AND IT SCORED 46/46. ***  `BASE_pass3b` came back CLEAN with the broken setup, because the row's\nonly oracle was the TEX decline count -- which garbage stores do not disturb.  **A row that passes\nwhile doing nothing it claims is exactly the vacuity the floor exists to prevent**, and no\nmeasurement could see it: the in-flight mutant run then scored `M_bit9` 46/46 ESCAPED, *which is\nwhat a no-op setup predicts*, and I would have blamed my choice of `PRIM_BAD` rather than my call.\n\n> **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, which is worth more than the typo: A CROSS-BIT ROW WHOSE ORACLE CANNOT\n> SEE ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS IS ONE TYPO AWAY FROM VACUOUS, PERMANENTLY.**  Counting only the TEX\n> decline cannot distinguish *\"ten latches fired and TEX still announced\"* from *\"the setup did\n> nothing and TEX announced\"*.  Both give 1.\n\n**REPAIRED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** the six calls now pass their value as `val`, and the row now\nASSERTS ITS OWN SETUP -- it counts how many of the eleven other announces actually appeared and\nwants all of them, so a silently-failing setup (a mistyped call, a moved register, a changed\nmessage) REDS the row instead of passing it.\n\n*** THE POINT ABOUT SEAT ROLES: THIS WAS FOUND BY READING, NOT BY MEASURING, AND NO MUTANT COULD\nHAVE FOUND IT. ***  Every mutant is scored against the same broken row, so the bug is invisible to\nthe entire kill table.  It took a seat reading the helper's signature against the call site.  *The\ncarrier's \"a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE\" understates it here:\nsome defects are visible ONLY to reading.*\n\n**codex also returned EIGHT further record defects on the same brief**, several in text this round\nwrote: the contract header still says an 8-byte `ld` \"STILL ENDS THE HOST\" one paragraph below my\ncorrection saying it does not; two probe sites still say \"every read arm here still exit(1)s\" and\n\"An 8-byte read still dies\"; my own \"No row below reads exit status\" is false of the `other decl`\nrow, which uses `how`; and \"these four TEX rows\" have the store-first shape when only two do.\n**Those are being fixed; they are listed here so the count is honest rather than rounded down.**\n\n*The brief was framed to avoid both known codex triggers -- \"no mutant, no evasion, no guest\nsequence\" -- and it answered with zero refusals.  That framing is now a proven third form alongside\ndiff review and source analysis.*\n\n*** THE LATCH-COLLISION CLASS IS CLOSED, MEASURED, WITH THE BEFORE/AFTER PRESERVED. ***\n\n    CLASSVERIFY.txt    M_bit6 45/46 KILLED   M_bit7 45/46 KILLED\n                       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  M_bit10 46/46 ESCAPED   <- disclosed at the time\n    CLASSVERIFY2       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  -- against the BROKEN row, so this is evidence of\n                       the store bug and NOT of an open cell\n    CLASSVERIFY3       M_bit9 45/46 KILLED   M_bit10 45/46 KILLED    <- fixed, self-checking row\n\n**Every one of the eleven other used bits of `d->warned` is now covered**: bits 0-5 by `M10`, bit 8\nby `tex/unmap` + `unmap/tex`, bits 6, 7, 9 and 10 by the widened `tex xbit`.\n\n*** AND `BASE_fixed` 46/46 MEANS MORE THAN THE EARLIER 46/46s. ***  The row wants `1/11` -- the TEX\ndecline once AND all eleven other announces present -- so a clean run now PROVES the setup fired.\nThe earlier clean runs did not: they scored a row whose six drawing-engine stores passed their VALUE\nas the OPCODE.\n\n**THE DISCLOSURE WAS WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED BY COVERAGE**, which also dissolves the rule conflict I\nhad escalated for the adjudication: the class-closure amendment permits disclosing uncovered cells,\nkimi and grok argued an in-closure cell on the edited line is a breach regardless, and rather than\nhave that adjudicated I closed the cells.  *The \"settling fact\" I had written down as unknown was\none grep away, in a shipped probe that already drives the drawing engine.*\n\n*** A WRONG RECORD IN A MEASUREMENT LOG, CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `CLASSVERIFY3.txt` carried its\ndriver's template footer from the previous run, saying bits 9 and 10 *\"ESCAPE BY DESIGN and are\nDISCLOSED\"* -- **directly above two KILLED results**.  A generated artifact whose boilerplate\ncontradicts its own numbers is the wrong-record class arriving in the one place a future round\ntrusts most.  *The driver writes that text unconditionally; the fix here is the artifact, and\nteaching the driver to derive its footer from the results is filed rather than done -- it is a\ntooling change owing its own controls.*\n\n*** PASS 4 -- THE CLOSING PASS.  FIVE SEATS, 3-2 TO CLOSE, AND I AM NOT CLOSING ON THE COUNT. ***\n\n    grok      YES, the chain closes -- 11/11, verified against the LIVE SOURCE\n    glm       CLOSE, conditionally -- 11/11\n    minimax   CHAIN CLOSES -- 11/11\n    deepseek  NO\n    kimi      DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED\n\n**Count the arguments, not the seats.**  Two of the objections do not survive and one does.\n\n## *** THE ONE THAT SURVIVES, AND IT TURNS THIS ROUND'S OWN DOCTRINE ON ME ***\n\nkimi: *\"against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants have a logged\nkill -- the other nine were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and\nthis round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills\nacross a row edit by construction.\"*\n\n**That is unanswerable.**  I proved that a probe edit can silently void a measurement, wrote it up as\nthis round's central lesson, and then presented nine kills carried across exactly such an edit.  Its\nformulation is the right one: **\"close every cell in ONE diff\" deserves \"kill every cell on ONE\nprobe version.\"**  Running as `SWEEP11` -- eleven aliases, one frozen probe, one table.\n\n## THE TWO THAT DO NOT SURVIVE, and why -- stated so the dissent is not lost\n\n* **deepseek: \"the widened row tests only one ordering for bits 6,7,9,10.\"**  The row's TEX load is\n  LAST, so under an alias the setup fires the bit FIRST and the TEX announce is suppressed -> count\n  0 -> red.  The mirror (TEX suppressing a setup announce) would show as `fired == 10` -> also red.\n  **A single-bit alias is symmetric; catching it in one ordering kills it.**  The bit-8 pair needed\n  both orderings because those are two DIFFERENT sites, not one bit.\n* **deepseek's Q2 escape** posits a session ordered TEX-read-first.  **The row's order is fixed in\n  the probe and is not reachable by a mutant of the DEVICE**, which is what the table perturbs.\n* **deepseek/kimi: \"enumeration exclusivity is not checkable from the packet.\"**  Correct about the\n  packet, and now SETTLED from source, twice independently -- my own derivation and grok's:\n  twelve enumerators `1<<0`..`1<<11`, no duplicates, no thirteenth, twelve latch sites all on\n  `d->warned`, no raw `|= 1<<k`, and no second bitmask in `struct sgi_re_data`.\n\n## *** A DEFECT INSIDE ONE OF MY CORRECTIONS, FOUND BY TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ***\n\ngrok and kimi both caught it.  My in-diff note said `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit` *\"would not redden\non an ACCEPT\"*.  **False: both oracle the decline-message COUNT, which an ACCEPT drives to 0, so both\ngo red.**  What they cannot do is distinguish an ACCEPT from a DELETED MESSAGE -- only the value pair\ncan.  **The correction UNDERSTATED the detection surface**, which is the same wrong-record class this\nround has spent the evening fixing, arriving inside the fix for it.  Corrected; and grok's\ndisposition is adopted -- *\"fix the sentence when convenient; do not reopen the chain for it.\"*\n\n**kimi's Q3 residual, kept because it is right and unfixed:** the self-check tests `lit in t`, which\nis EMISSION not LATCHING, and the literals are unanchored substrings.  So the `/11` half proves the\nmessages appeared, not that any bit was set.  For this class that is harmless -- the kill signal is\nTEX's own count -- but the contract should say so rather than imply the row verifies latching.\n\n*** THE CHAIN CLOSES.  ELEVEN OF ELEVEN CELLS KILLED ON ONE PROBE VERSION, WITH THE DENOMINATOR.\nSWEEP11.txt. ***\n\n    SWEEP_BASE   46/46   the final self-checking probe, CLEAN\n    bits 0-7, 9, 10      45/46 each   red: `tex xbit`\n    bit 8                43/46        red: `tex xbit`, `tex/unmap`, `unmap/tex`\n    NOT KILLED: none\n\n**kimi's pass-4 objection is discharged BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument.**  It held that only 2 of\n11 aliases had a logged kill against the FINAL row, and that the other nine were carried across a\nrow edit -- which this round's own doctrine forbids, having proved that a probe edit can silently\nvoid a measurement.  **Every cell is now killed on one frozen probe, in one run.**\n\n*** BIT 8 IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW IN THE TABLE, AND IT WAS NOT DESIGNED TO BE. ***  It reds\nTHREE rows rather than one, because `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` is exactly what the two pass-2 ordering rows\ntarget.  **So a single mutant demonstrates that the pass-2 repair and the pass-3 class closure are\nboth live and INDEPENDENT on the same probe version** -- which no separate table could have shown,\nbecause each was previously measured against its own instrument.\n\n**THE CLOSING PASS IS VALID BY ITS OWN RULE:** the diff was delivered and inlined, the roster is\nrecorded with its misses named (codex deliberately not fired -- the ask-again shape), and the zero\ncarries its denominator -- **eleven cells, enumerated from the twelve used bits of `d->warned`,\nderived from source twice independently rather than asserted.**\n\n*What closed it was not the 3-2 vote.  Three seats said close and two did not; both dissents were\nadjudicated on their arguments -- one refuted from source, one right and now measured away.\nCounting seats would have closed the chain an hour early on a denominator of 2 of 11.*\n\n*** THIS ROW'S CENSUS IS STALE BY A FACTOR OF TWO, AND THE BLOCKER IT IS BUILT AROUND IS ALREADY\nDISCHARGED.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here twice. ***\n\ncodex, on the current files: *\"The current files contradict the historical counts: I find SIX active\n`exit(1)` statements, and the probe's six former death rows have already been replaced.\"*\n\n**DERIVED, not transcribed -- and my FIRST attempt was wrong, which is worth recording because it is\nthe deriver rule landing on me again.**  A line-prefix test (*does this line start with a comment\nmarker?*) reported SEVEN, because `dev_sgi_re.c:259` is a CONTINUATION line of a block comment that\nbegins with neither `/*` nor `*`.  **I classified by a CORRELATE of being commented rather than by\nbeing commented.**  Stripping block and line comments properly, preserving line numbers:\n\n    ACTIVE exit( in dev_sgi_re.c:  6      :1463  :1471  :1547  :1552  :1563  :1685\n    (six further `exit(' texts are inside comments, several of them prose ABOUT this very row)\n\n**So the title's \"TWELVE exit(1) STATEMENTS and ELEVEN GUEST-REACHABLE KILLS\" is stale by 2x.**  The\nintervening rounds -- `#497`'s width de-fatalisation and `#499`'s work in the same file -- removed\nor commented six of them, and this row's headline was never re-derived.\n\n*** THE MORE IMPORTANT HALF: THE ORDERING OBLIGATION THIS ROW IS ORGANISED AROUND NO LONGER EXISTS. ***\n\nThe row says, in capitals: *\"FIXING sgirekills WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, SIX ROWS'\nWORTH ... it therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST.\"*\n\n**MEASURED against the shipped probe:**\n\n    grep -c 'ld kill'                        regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   ->  5\n    grep -n 'row(' ... | grep -i kill        ->  ZERO row() calls.  All five hits are COMMENTS,\n                                                 and both are written in the PAST TENSE:\n        :370  \"THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + \\\" ld kill\\\", how + txt, \\\"exit1+txt\\\")' -- they asserted\"\n        :527  \"THE OLD ROW was `row(\\\"other kill\\\", how, \\\"exit1\\\")' -- DEATH-ONLY.\"\n\n**There are no death-only rows left to re-pin.**  A later round already replaced them -- the probe's\nown header says so at `:33`: *\"CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER\nEXIST\"*.  So the oracle-correction ordering this row inherits is **already satisfied**, and the\nsequencing constraint that made it look expensive is gone.\n\n> **THE ROW IS THEREFORE CHEAPER AND SMALLER THAN IT READS, AND BOTH ERRORS POINT THE SAME WAY:\n> toward over-stating the work.**  A future round reading the headline plans for eleven kills and a\n> six-row re-pin; the real job is six kills with no re-pin owed.\n\n*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN COUNT RULE, EARNED RATHER THAN QUOTED: \"the rounds that consult the\nnumber are the rounds that change it.\"  `#497` and `#499` both edited this file, and each left this\nrow's headline one step further from the source. ***\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and codex said so itself:** it could give *\"a defensive census and\nguard-order analysis\"* but *\"can't map guest-controlled values or access sequences to\nhost-termination paths\"* from the three permitted files.  **So the count of ACTIVE exits is\nmeasured; the count of GUEST-REACHABLE ones is not**, and the title's \"eleven guest-reachable\" has no\ncurrent replacement figure.  The row should carry the six with their guards and leave reachability\nopen until it is measured, rather than swapping one unverified number for another.",
