tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28957-   ],
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28958-   "hold_question": "The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28959:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` §7i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28960-  },
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28961-  {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28962-   "id": "exit0oracle",
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28966-   "commits": "",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28967-   "title": "shipped rows pin survival as an `exit0` want, so a mutant that announces and then exit(0)s satisfies them -- count DERIVED, never restated: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28968-   "witness": "harness:READ by the codex seat, 2026-08-31, against regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28969:   "note": "*** A SURVIVAL ORACLE THAT A WRONG `exit(0)` SATISFIES IS NOT A SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  Found by the codex seat while answering `sgideprimop`'s pass-1 brief, in shipped material it was not asked about.  `regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py` pins host survival with `row(..., how, \"exit0\", ...)` at **NINE sites across FIVE probe files**, derived rather than transcribed with `grep -rn 'how, \\\"exit0\\\"' regress/*.py`:  `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4 (`lo_alive`, `lo_alive2`, `hi_alive`, `hi_rd_alive`), `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1.  *codex named three, in the one file it had been given; the class is three times that and spans devices it was never shown -- which is why the row is filed as a CLASS and the deriver is written down instead of the number.*  The oracle reads the process's exit status after the harness sends `quit`.  **An emulator that calls `exit(0)` on the guest access under test ALSO exits 0**, so the row cannot distinguish *the host survived and was then asked to quit* from *the host died quietly at exactly the moment the row exists to observe*.  **THE REPAIR codex NAMES:** assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally followed by a post-trigger command whose result proves the emulator was still answering.  The probe already captures `alive` -- the rows simply do not use it.  *** RANKED AS COVERAGE DEBT, NOT AN L2, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE DOCUMENTED ONE. ***  These rows are in a SHIPPED detector that sits above the vacuity floor: it fails on the pre-fix build, on a verbatim reinstatement (14/25, eleven reds) and on a null medium.  The stopping rule files findings against shipped material and fixes only MEASURED FALSE PASSES and WRONG RECORDS.  **No mutant has been built that exploits this**, so it is a hypothetical edit in an uncovered cell -- filed, ranked below live defects in the same file.  *What would promote it to L2: a measured mutant that calls `exit(0)` on the declined path and sweeps the detector clean.  That is one build and is the natural first job for whoever takes this row.*  **CHECK THE CLASS BEFORE FIXING THE INSTANCE** -- `grep -rn 'exit0' regress/*.py` across the tree, since the idiom will have been copied; this project has twice corrected an instance and left its siblings standing.  *** THE COUNT IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 9, AND THE POPULATION IS 13.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by the adjudicating seat, which could not reproduce my figure by any method. ***  **THE DERIVER, and it is the fix as well as the count:** `grep -rn '\"exit0\"' regress/*.py`, counting `row(...)` want-sites -- **13 across 5 files**: `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 5, `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4, `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1 (that one INSIDE A LOOP, so its runtime rows exceed its textual site).  **My grep was `'how, \"exit0\"'` -- anchored on the VARIABLE NAME rather than the ORACLE VALUE -- and `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` uses `how1`, `how2`, `how3`, `how4`.** Four sites invisible to a pattern that looked precise.  *A row filed to adjudicate oracle blindness shipped with a count produced by a blind pattern; that is the frozen-number rule arriving inside the filing that names it.*  *** THE RANKING SURVIVES, AND THE SEAT GAVE IT A MEASURED REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THE BLINDNESS IS PER-ROW, NOT PER-DETECTOR. ***  `lo_sent` reads `printed(t, \"v0\") if alive else \"died\"`, so it reds on ANY death whatever the exit code -- verified by the seat, which built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found it KILLED by the read-side sentinel rows even though all four `*_alive` rows passed it.  **So no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole (not L2), and no record is made false where a session-mate positive row exists (not L3).**  Coverage debt, filed, med -- confirmed, on evidence rather than on the stopping rule alone.  **THE FIXING ROUND'S FIRST JOB IS THEREFORE A NARROWER CENSUS: which of the 13 lack a death-proof SESSION-MATE?**  Those are the live ones; the rest are redundant belt-and-braces and can be repaired cheaply or left.  *That is a smaller and better-aimed job than \"repair 13 rows\".*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l = 24 on 2026-08-31\n\n*** THE COUNT WENT STALE WITHIN HOURS OF BEING FILED, BY THE FILER'S OWN EDIT. ***  Filed 2026-08-31 as THIRTEEN; derived the SAME DAY as TWENTY-FOUR, of which sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py alone holds ELEVEN because #499 pass 4 added seven.  That is this project's documented pattern exactly -- the rounds that consult a number are the rounds that change it -- so the title now carries the DERIVER and no literal.  (Demoted: 13, filed 2026-08-31, never a current claim.)  Per-file on that date: sgi_re_unmapped 11, gdrom_toclen 5, sgi_de_width 4, gdrom_decline 2, gdrom_default 1, sgi_re_width 1.\n\nAND THE CLASS BEARS ON #499's OWN PASS-4 ROWS.  Of the twelve rows pass 4 added, five are SURVIVAL-ONLY with an `exit0` want.  The READ side is covered -- `clip rd sent` reads `died` when the host is gone.  The WRITE side has NO post-death positive observable: surv wants exit0 and is satisfied, decl already counted its message before the exit.  A WRITE-SIDE-ONLY exit(0) is therefore PREDICTED to escape all 48 rows.  That is clause (d) shaped -- guard kept, its repair (falling through to the common tail) removed -- so it is NOT filed on my reading; it is QUEUED AS MUTANT E0W and will be measured.  Floor clause (b) is unaffected and measured green: a verbatim exit(1) reinstatement scores 18/48.\n\nAND A SECOND SITE FOUND THE SAME DAY BY THE lcabadval SEAT, which makes this a CLASS rather than a probe-local habit: on `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` (dev_lca.c) are bare fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert or deassert kills the host with the SAME exit status as the value guards under test.  Any lcabadval row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM, never the exit status -- an exit-status oracle there cannot tell the defect from the confound.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28970-   "entries": [
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28971-    {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28972-     "seat": "codex",
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29009-   "witness": "probe:regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py -- RUNG 3, MEASURED 2026-09-07 on the COMMITTED build (a264a34), unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1'.  HEAD 4/6: `kill width' reads 0x0 where 0x100000000 is required, `kill notzero' reds because 0x0 is a value the guard ACCEPTS.  Four controls green.  Artifact: regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/probe_head_20260907.out",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29010-   "note": "*** FILED AS ONE SITE IN dev_lca.c; CENSUSED THE SAME DAY AND IT IS A CLASS.  Widened low -> med. ***\n\nTHE DERIVERS -- run them, do not trust these numbers, and note they answer DIFFERENT questions:\n\n    # (1) the whole cast population -- mostly HARMLESS and NOT the defect\n    grep -rc '(int)idata\\|(int) idata' src/devices/*.c | grep -v ':0$'\n\n    # (2) the sharp sub-class: a cast inside a diagnostic at a site that then EXITS\n    #     (a python window scan; see the round record for the exact form)\n\nOn 2026-08-31 (1) gave 309 casts across 70 device files and (2) gave 45 host-kill sites, about twenty of which carry an explicit full-width guard on idata.\n\n*** MOST OF THE 309 ARE NOT DEFECTS, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT -- an 8- or 16-bit register loses nothing through (int). ***  The defect needs BOTH halves:\n  * a guard that tests the FULL 64-bit value -- `idata & ~MASK' or `idata != CONST'; and\n  * a diagnostic that prints the TRUNCATED one.\nThen a guest write whose only distinguishing bits are above bit 31 dies, while the message reports a value that would have been accepted.\n\n*** THE SHARPEST INSTANCE, VERIFIED BY COMPUTATION AND BY READING THE ARM: dev_pvr.c's `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata != 0x0cff0000)' paired with `(int) idata'. ***  A guest writing 0x1_0cff0000 fails the 64-bit compare and the host dies -- and the message prints 0x0cff0000, **EXACTLY THE VALUE THE CODE SAYS IS ACCEPTABLE**.  So the diagnostic tells a maintainer the guest wrote the correct value and died for it, which sends the investigation in precisely the wrong direction.  dev_lca.c's `idata & ~1' is the same shape, milder: it prints 0x0 for a value whose low half is genuinely zero.\n\nSEVERITY REASONING, stated rather than assumed: none of this changes WHETHER the host dies -- the guards are unchanged and the kills are the kills.  It changes only what the log says about WHY, at the one moment the log is all that survives.  That is why it is med and not high, and why it is a RECORDS-CORRECTNESS defect in product code rather than a control-flow one.\n\nNOT A ONE-LINE SWEEP.  Each site needs its own read: the fix is the format specifier plus a (long long) cast, but which sites are genuinely reachable with a >32-bit datum depends on the register width and the guest paths that reach it, and a blanket edit to 45 diagnostics is exactly the unlicensed addition this project refuses.  The honest first round is the ~20 sites whose guard is demonstrably full-width, ordered by whether a guest can deliver a >32-bit write at all.\n\nFound while checking whether the dev_lca.c site was a one-off, as parallel work during a gate run.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29011-   "entries": [],
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29012:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** THE WITNESS HAD A ROW THAT COULD NOT FAIL, AND A PASS-2 REVIEW OF THE ROUND FOUND IT.\nREPAIRED AND CONTROLLED, 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe rows tested `\"IOC_CONF: 0x100000000\" in text` -- a SUBSTRING -- and manufactured the displayed\n`got` from the boolean.  codex: *\"Append one literal 0 before the diagnostic's newline.  The message\nbecomes 0x1000000000, yet `kill width` still reports a green 0x100000000.\"*  `zero` had the mirror\nflaw (`0x0abc` contains `0x0`).\n\n> **That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape INSIDE THE WITNESS FOR THE DEFECT IT EXISTS TO PIN** --\n> and it survived my own review, a design review, and the run.  It was found only by asking a seat\n> what edit keeps the witness green.\n\n**REPAIRED** to parse the number and compare integers, reporting the value actually found.\n**CONTROLLED in both directions:**\n\n    fixed build, repaired oracle          6/6      kill width 0x100000000\n    codex's escape (TRAILING 0)           5/6      kill width 0x1000000000  RED\n\n*** AND MY FIRST CONTROL TESTED THE WRONG MUTANT, which is worth recording because it would have\nbeen a false proof. ***  I applied the extra `0` BEFORE the value (`0x0%\" PRIx64`), giving\n`0x0100000000` -- **numerically identical to `0x100000000`**, so the row passed correctly and proved\nnothing.  That is the carrier's own *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT* rule, case 1: the mutant\nis mis-built.  **The leading-zero form is a genuine EQUIVALENT mutant** and is recorded as such\nrather than as an escape.  The trailing-zero form is codex's, and it reds.\n\n*Artifacts: `probe_fix_newora_20260907.out`, `probe_mutant_trailzero_20260907.out`, and the\nmis-built `probe_mutant_extrazero_20260907.out` kept as the record of the equivalence.*\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/devices/dev_lca.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/devices/dev_lca.c\n                                   contains the `#506' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29013-   "commits": "7a41b3f"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29014-  },
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-29015-  {
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31284-   "sev": "low",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31285-   "state": "open",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31286-   "opened": "2026-09-06",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31287:   "witness": "harness:source census, codex GPT-6 Astra at max, regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_irqasserted_20260906.txt",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31288:   "state_note": "*** SEVERITY CORRECTED HIGH -> LOW THE SAME EVENING IT WAS FILED, BY THE PASS IT WAS FILED PENDING. ***  I filed this HIGH off ONE GREP while the investigation was in flight.  The census was right; the severity was not, and the correction is recorded rather than the row quietly re-graded.\n\n**VERDICT (codex, GPT-6 Astra at max): REAL GAP in all three, and ALL THREE OF MY PREMISES HOLD.**  Six families set the flag, exactly three are consumed, and alpha/i960/riscv have no reader.  There is no alternate delivery mechanism for any of the three.\n\n**BUT THE THREE ARE NOT ALIKE, AND THAT IS WHAT MOVES THE SEVERITY:**\n* **i960 -- DEAD SCAFFOLDING.**  `cpu_i960_instr.c`'s instruction switch contains ONLY `default: goto bad;`. The FIRST instruction stops as unimplemented, so no boot, no timer programming, no driver wait is ever reached.  There is no guest to miss an interrupt.  `i960_exception()` is likewise a stopping stub.\n* **riscv -- DEAD SCAFFOLDING.**  `cpu_riscv_instr.c` implements compressed NOP/ADDI only and the 32-bit branch goes unconditionally to `bad`.  `machine_test.c` DOES wire RTC -> interrupt controller -> CPU, so an asserted request really does end at the unused flag -- but this instruction subset cannot boot an OS or program that timer.\n* **alpha -- A GENUINE HOLE, and the finding is that the PORT IS INCOMPLETE.**  `cpu_alpha_palcode.c` stores entry vectors and IPL (`PAL_OSF1_wrent`, `PAL_OSF1_swpipl`) and **`kentry` has no reader anywhere**; `PAL_OSF1_rti` appears only in the naming function with no execution implementation.  So delivery is missing at the CPU/PAL level, not merely at the flag.\n\n**DISPOSITIONS:** removing the field and its stores is reasonable scaffold cleanup for i960 and riscv, and adding a dispatch check alone would achieve no usable capability in either.  For alpha the item is *track completion of the CPU/PAL/chipset path*, which subsumes `lcabadval`'s two interrupt stubs: **removing those exits still leaves both upstream forwarding and CPU delivery unfinished.**\n\n*** AND IT CORRECTED MY CENSUS IN A WAY THAT DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONCLUSION BUT DOES CHANGE THE PICTURE: `cpu_dyntrans.c` ALSO delivers MIPS and SH interrupts, through DIFFERENTLY NAMED STATE. ***  My grep keyed on one identifier and so could not see them.  That is the correlate-not-the-class shape the carrier names -- the output was a plausible number either way -- and it is exactly the risk the brief disclosed about itself.\n\n*** THE SHARPEST FINDING IS ABOUT MY BRIEF, NOT THE CODE: the seat named *\"at least one numbered premise must be wrong\"* AS THE UNSUPPORTED ASSUMPTION. ***  The three-question set closes with *WHICH IS WRONG?  Assume at least one is*, which is a good adversarial device and IS an unwarranted premise when the premises happen to be sound.  A seat that answers *none, and here is why the forcing assumption is unjustified* is giving the correct answer, and the template should say so rather than pressing for a defect that may not exist.  Filed against the template as `briefforcedwrong`.\n\nHonest limit the seat stated and this row keeps: these are source-derived consequences, with no guest boot run, and \"dead scaffolding\" means the capability is absent rather than relocated.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31289-   "entries": [
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31290-    {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31291-     "date": "2026-09-06",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31292-     "phase": "research",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31293-     "seat": "codex",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31294:     "note": "VERDICT: REAL GAP in alpha, i960 and riscv; all three premises correct as written.  Distinguished the three: i960 and riscv are DEAD SCAFFOLDING (neither can execute a second instruction -- i960's switch is only `default: goto bad;', riscv implements compressed NOP/ADDI and sends the 32-bit branch to `bad'), so there is no guest to miss an interrupt and the disposition is scaffold cleanup; alpha is a GENUINE HOLE at the CPU/PAL level (`kentry' stored with no reader, `PAL_OSF1_rti' named but unimplemented), so removing lcabadval's two interrupt exits still leaves forwarding AND delivery unfinished.  **Corrected my census**: cpu_dyntrans.c also delivers MIPS and SH interrupts through differently-named state, which a single-identifier grep could not see.  **And named my brief's forcing question as its own unsupported assumption** -- filed as briefforcedwrong.  Answer preserved at regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_irqasserted_20260906.txt"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31295-    }
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31296-   ]
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31297-  },
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31303-   "witness": null,
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31304-   "title": "BRIEF_TEMPLATE's closing question asserts that a premise must be wrong -- an unsupported assumption when they are sound",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31305-   "hold_question": "Should the three-question set's `WHICH IS WRONG?  Assume at least one is' be softened to permit `none, and the forcing assumption is unwarranted' as a first-class answer?  It is a measured-effective adversarial device (it has found four wrong premises in one brief) AND it is literally an unsupported claim about the brief's own content.  Both are true; which wins?",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31306:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 by the codex seat, against the brief template rather than against any code.  Answering the `irqassertedunread' brief it wrote: *\"Thus the unsupported assumption is 'at least one numbered premise must be wrong.'\"*  All three premises were in fact correct.\n\n**The device earns its place and that is not in dispute** -- the carrier records it finding FOUR wrong premises in a single brief, and tonight it produced three real premise corrections on `lcabadval`.  The objection is narrower: as WORDED it instructs the seat to assume a defect exists, so a seat with sound premises must either invent one or push back on the instruction.  Pushing back is the right answer and the template does not invite it.\n\n**Proposed wording, for the adjudicator rather than applied here:** keep the question and add *`or say NONE and name the forcing assumption as the defect'*.  Held rather than edited, because the template is the artifact every brief starts from and a change to it changes every future panel -- which the carrier says is an owner decision, not a round's.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31307-   "entries": []
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31308-  },
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31309-  {
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31313-   "opened": "2026-09-06",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31314-   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel(): DE_MODE_LIN_B -- a DOCUMENTED NetBSD mode -- falls to the default arm, which assigns *color = random(); a copy then writes that random value to the destination",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31315-   "witness": "probe:regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py -- rung 3 on unmodified -E sgi -e ip32, 8 rows, liveness AND device-signature controls green; re-pinned oracle measured RED at 7/8 on the COMMITTED pre-fix build (regress/artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/prefix_repinned_RED.out), 8/8 after.  Gate-wired into gate_arm.sh; 880 checks PASS.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31316:   "state_note": "*** RAISED THE SAME EVENING IT WAS FILED, ON A READING THAT MADE IT WORSE: THE UNHANDLED VALUE IS A DOCUMENTED\nMODE, NOT A NONSENSE ONE. ***  Verified against the vendored header before this edit.\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()` extracts `tlb_mode = (mode >> 10) & 0x7` and switches on it.  The function's own\ncomment lists five NetBSD modes, and `src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h` confirms their values:\n\n    DE_MODE_TLB_A  0x0000 -> 0  handled       DE_MODE_LIN_A  0x1000 -> 4  handled\n    DE_MODE_TLB_B  0x0400 -> 1  handled       DE_MODE_LIN_B  0x1400 -> 5  *** default -> *color = random() ***\n    DE_MODE_TLB_C  0x0800 -> 2  handled       (3, 6 and 7 also reach it)\n\n**FOUR OF THE FIVE DOCUMENTED MODES ARE IMPLEMENTED AND THE FIFTH RETURNS RANDOM DATA.**  So this is not a\nguest choosing an out-of-spec value; it is a guest choosing one of the five modes the vendored header defines.\n\n**THE GUEST-VISIBLE PATH, in `draw_primitive()`, and both mode words are GUEST-WRITABLE registers**\n(`CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC` / `CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` via `d->de_reg[]`):\n\n    if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)\n            horrible_getputpixel(0, ..., &color, src_mode);   // GET may set color = random()\n    ...\n    if (draw)\n            horrible_getputpixel(1, ..., &color, dst_mode);   // PUT writes it\n\nA failed SOURCE read and the DESTINATION write are the same `color` variable in the same loop iteration, so\nthe random value is written out per pixel with no decline.  **The code's own comment on that XFER branch reads\n*\"Pixel colors copied from another source.  (OpenBSD draws characters using this mechanism.)\"*** -- so the\nbranch is one a real guest driver uses, which is why this is filed HIGH rather than as a curiosity.\n\n**STILL OWED AND NOT CLAIMED: the rung-3 witness.**  Reachability is READ, not MEASURED -- I have shown the\nconstant exists, the extraction reaches 5, and the registers are writable, and NOT that any in-tree guest\nprograms LIN_B.  A witness-attempt brief is with codex asking exactly that, including the awkward part: the\noracle cannot pin a value because `random()` is non-deterministic, so it must pin *the destination differs\nfrom the source* and *two identical runs differ*.\n\n*** THE PROVENANCE IS THE LESSON: this came from asking what a DISABLED kill FELL BACK TO. ***  It is the\nsecond such finding today -- `lcabadval`'s disabled kill leaves a live `dev = 0` that aliases 'no selected\ndevice' to device zero.  Both look like a decision already taken and neither is one to copy.  Four exits in\nthis file are commented out; the other three still owe that question.\n\n*** RUNG-3 ATTEMPT MADE, AND THE ANSWER IS WITNESS BUILDABLE (grok).  The probe is SPECIFIED, NOT RUN --\nthat seat is plan-mode, so this is a READING and the measurement is still owed. ***\n\n**THE FALSE NEGATIVE THAT WOULD HAVE WASTED THE FIRST ATTEMPT:** at reset the destination mode is TLB_A and\n`re_tlb_a[]` has bit 31 clear, so the PUT returns WITHOUT WRITING.  A naive probe -- set SRC to LIN_B, draw,\nread the destination -- finds it unchanged and reports NO DEFECT.  Using LIN_A as the destination closes it,\nsince case 4 forces the valid bit.  `sgi_de_primop_probe.py` had already named that gap.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE IS CERTAIN, NOT PROBABILISTIC:** glibc `random()` is 31-bit, so the destination's bit 31 is\nclear and inequality with a source of `0xAABBCCDD` is guaranteed rather than 2^-32.  Two runs differ by\ndefault seeding; `-D` makes them identical, which changes the second oracle and not the defect.\n\n*** THE SEVERITY SPLIT GROK NAMED, AND IT IS THE HONEST LIMIT OF THIS ROW: `LIN_B` IS DOCUMENTED AND STORABLE\nBUT NOT SHOWN TO BE DRIVER-SELECTED. ***  It appears only in `crmfbreg.h` and this function's comment; the\nXFER comment names OpenBSD character drawing without saying which source mode; the PROM's `MTE_TLB_LIN_A/B`\nzero-fill path does NOT call `horrible_getputpixel`; and `images.md` has no O2/sgimips image to boot.  **A\ndocumented-but-unused mode and a mode a driver really selects are different severities**, and only the\nconstant's existence is established here.\n\n*** THE WITNESS IS BUILT AND MEASURED.  7/7 on the committed build, artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/head_79cd12e.out. ***\n\nReal guest instructions on an unmodified committed `-E sgi -e ip32', no `device_add'.  Both mandated controls\ngreen: LIVENESS (store then load through RAM) and DEVICE SIGNATURE (CRIME_DE_STATUS reads 0x1e000000 where\nRAM reads 0).  The value written was 0x4b1763a1 on one run and 0x2ee4b4df on the next, **bit 31 CLEAR both\ntimes** -- glibc random() is 31-bit, so inequality with a source of 0xaabbccdd is CERTAIN rather than 2^-32,\nwhich is what makes the oracle deterministic despite the value not being.\n\n*** TWO TRAPS HIT WHILE BUILDING IT, AND THE ACCEPT CONTROL IS WHAT CAUGHT THE FIRST. ***\n* **The destination tile pointer was 0, so every pixel went to PHYSICAL ADDRESS 0.**  grok had named exactly\n  this hazard -- *'do not leave it 0: that writes phys 0'* -- and I hit it anyway from a direction it had also\n  named: a 64-bit `sd' writes the HIGH word to `[n]' and the low word to `[n+1]', so storing 0x10 gave\n  `re_linear_a[0] = 0, [1] = 0x10'.  **A probe with only the defect rows would have read the destination\n  unchanged and reported NO DEFECT.**  The ACCEPT control failed instead, which is what an accept row is for.\n* `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS into a 64-bit register, so the source value read back as 0xffffffffaabbccdd and the row\n  failed on FORMATTING rather than behaviour.  The shipped `sgi_re_width_probe.py` carries that normalisation\n  and says why; this probe should have copied it and now does.\n\n**THE ORACLE MUST INVERT AFTER THE FIX, and the order is not optional.**  These rows PASS while the defect is\nPRESENT, so post-fix they assert the wrong thing.  The oracle-correction rule requires the re-pin FIRST, the\ncommitted build measured RED under the new expectations, and the code changed only THEN -- otherwise the\nre-pin cannot be told from fitting the test to the binary.  Design panel is running; the post-fix row set\ndepends on its answer to what `*color' should be on a failed get.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 1: THE PANEL CONVERGES ON MEMBER 3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE), 3 of 5 answering seats,\nAND THE TWO DISSENTS ARE REFUTED ON A POINT BOTH FILE-READING SEATS REACHED INDEPENDENTLY. ***\n\ndeepseek and minimax voted MEMBER 5 (FAULT).  **The device access that triggers this ALREADY SUCCEEDED** --\nthe guest's store to PRIMITIVE|START was accepted -- so there is nothing left to fault, and raising a bus\nerror would report a failure for an access the hardware acknowledges.  grok put it exactly: *'member 5 is\navailable here' is true of IP32 and FALSE OF THIS CALL SITE.*  Their shared argument -- that only FAULT stops\nthe bogus write -- is true of a weaker member 3 than either file-reading seat proposed.\n\n**THE REMAINING QUESTION IS SCOPE, and it is decided by the round-scope rule rather than by vote:**\n* **codex** wants the complete fix now: an explicit success result on the function, and every GET site\n  (source, the ROP `oldcolor', MTE's `bg') taught to suppress its dependent PUT.\n* **grok** wants the minimal one: delete the `random()' assignment, let the PUT write `fg', and file skip-PUT\n  and a real LIN_B case as separate rounds.\n**A return value plus seven call sites is not ONE SITE**, and the reopening rule says a round takes one.\ngrok's scoping is taken; codex's obligations are filed rather than dropped.\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS GROK'S AND IT DISCRIMINATES EVERY CANDIDATE AT ONCE, which is why it is worth having even\nthough the fix is minimal: *** zero leaves `0`, skip-PUT leaves the pre-paint `0x11111111`, IMPLEMENT-as-copy\nleaves `0xaabbccdd`, and untouched-with-PUT leaves `fg`.  **THE PROBE MUST THEREFORE PROGRAM `fg` TO A\nDISTINCTIVE NON-ZERO VALUE** -- the witness ran with `fg=0x0`, where 'untouched' and 'zeroed' are the same\nobservation and the oracle would be vacuous.  `lin_b written` is DROPPED as a defect row: under this member it\nis true before and after.\n\n*** PASS 2: SHIP, SIX SEATS ANSWERING, NONE WALLED, NONE DOWN -- the fullest roster of the session, and it cost one liveness call to get there. ***\n\nThree explicit SHIP verdicts (kimi, glm, minimax); both FILE-READING seats answered without a verdict line and both were read in full, which is where the one finding was.  **codex re-derived all 40 row outcomes (8 rows x 5 mutants) from the preserved artifacts and confirmed the table**, saying plainly it ran nothing itself.  **grok answered NONE IS WRONG to the five-way question and named the question's own frame as the unsupported assumption** -- the second seat tonight to make that correction independently.\n\n**THE ONE L4, AND IT WAS MINE:** `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP`'s comment still claimed `1 << 10` was the next free bit and that the enum ended there -- false the moment `SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10` was appended below it.  The file warns about exactly this collision four lines earlier, having paid for it once; I read that warning, placed my bit correctly by it, and left the signpost pointing at the bit I had taken.  Fixed in-round:\nthe superseded sentence is DEMOTED inside its own correction, so exactly one live next-free claim remains and it sits on the last enumerator.\n\n**WHAT BOTH FILE-READING SEATS AGREE IS NOT COVERED**, recorded as the contract's disclosure rather than discovered later: line and MTE execution paths, other mode combinations, junk modes 3/6/7, the dest-side LIN_B PUT, MTE's `&bg' GET, the colour-mode default arm, and resistance to latch-sharing mutants (disclosed 8/8 in isolation, because nothing in these sessions sets the other bit first).\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-06 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nReading, not measurement: verified the default arm executes `*color = random()`, that LIN_B extracts to tlb_mode 5 and is unhandled while the other four documented modes are handled, that both mode words come from guest-writable de_reg entries, and that the failed source GET and the destination PUT share one `color` variable in one loop iteration.  Rung-3 witness attempt fired at codex.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-06 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nWITNESS BUILT AND GREEN, 7/7 on HEAD 79cd12e, from grok's specification.  Its design credit is specific: the warning that a naive probe finds nothing because the reset destination mode has its valid bit clear was the difference between a witness and a false negative -- and the accept control it implied is what caught my own instance of that trap.  Probe frozen at artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/probe.frozen.py (md5 a734e524).  Kill table NOT started: the fix design comes first, because the oracle has to invert and the oracle-correction rule fixes the order.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (regress stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nGATE WIRING -- and **the gate's first run FAILED on the block I had just written**, which is the padded-column\ntrap in its MATCHES-TWO-ROWS direction: `FAIL sgi_re randompixel row: lin_b diag  got=2 want=1` while the probe\nemitted 8 rows, 8 green, 0 red.  The probe was right and the CHECK was wrong -- `\"^$v .*ok$\"` with ONE space\nmatches `lin_b diag once' as well as `lin_b diag'.  **The sibling block eight lines above uses TWO spaces**; I\ncopied its comments and its two lessons and not its pattern.\n\nFixed and PRE-FLIGHTED against the real probe log before re-running the gate -- the two-second check the carrier\nprescribes for this exact trap.  Both directions measured: the fixed pattern selects exactly 1 per row (SUM=8),\nand the negative control still reproduces `got=2` under the old form.\n\n**The margin is recorded in the comment because the other direction is SILENT.**  The probe prints \"%-16s\" plus\nan explicit space, so a name of N chars is followed by (16-N)+1 spaces; the longest here is `lin_b diag once' at\n15, leaving exactly two.  A name of 16+ makes the two-space pattern UNSATISFIABLE and the check stays GREEN while\ntesting nothing.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-07 in commit 257f56f -- AND SECTION L HAD TO TELL ME, WHICH IS THE POINT. ***\n\n`precommit_check.sh` section L refused the NEXT commit with:\n\n    HARD   257f56f   sgirandompixel open   work shipped and the row does not name it (commits EMPTY)\n\n**That is the UNRECORDED ROUND failure `BRIEF_TEMPLATE` section C0 exists to prevent** -- a round ships, the\nledger freezes at its pre-ship state, and a later loop treats the row as unfinished and re-panels it.  The\ntemplate records that costing NINE SEATS on `dev_wdc.c`.\n\n**I wrote about that failure in this same session and then committed it one commit later.**  The gate caught\nit in the very next precommit, which is exactly the difference between a rule in prose and a rule in a check:\nthe `#488` instance went undetected until a full panel had been convened on work that had already shipped.\n\nRecorded here rather than fixed silently, because a row that quietly gains a SHA looks the same afterwards\nwhether the author noticed or the gate did.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31317-   "entries": [
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31318-    {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31319-     "date": "2026-09-06",
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31487-   "opened": "2026-09-06",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31488-   "title": "codex content-filter refusal survived neutralisation -- the trigger is the REACH-THE-DEFECT framing, not the subject; and its signature reads as an ANSWER through panel.sh",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31489-   "witness": "harness:two refusals of the same brief, before and after phrase neutralisation",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31490:   "state_note": "*** MY OWN DIAGNOSIS IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE IT CHANGES\nTHE REMEDY. ***  I wrote that the trigger is 'the SUBJECT SHAPE, not a phrase', on the evidence of two\nrefusals of one brief.  **codex then answered a brief on THE SAME DEFECT, in the same file, at 167 KB.**\n\nThe difference is the FRAMING, and it is sharper than either earlier guess:\n  REFUSED  the WITNESS brief -- 'can you build a probe that REACHES this and shows the bad value?'\n  ANSWERED the DESIGN brief -- 'what should this code do instead, and what should the rows assert?'\nSame defect, same file, same session.  A request to CONSTRUCT A PATH TO A DEFECT reads as an attack recipe;\na request to DECIDE WHAT THE CODE SHOULD DO reads as ordinary review.  Neutralising the vocabulary did not\nhelp because the vocabulary was never the trigger.\n\n**SO THE REMEDY IS NOT ONLY RE-ROUTING: it is to ask codex the DESIGN question and let another seat answer\nthe REACHABILITY one.**  That is cheaper than it sounds -- grok answered the witness brief with a complete\nbuildable sketch, and codex then produced the best design answer of the panel.  Each seat was asked the\nquestion it would answer.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CHECK HAZARD IS UNCHANGED AND IS THE PART THAT WAS ACTUALLY FIXED. ***  The refusal writes a\nzero-byte stdout with the reason on stderr on a direct call -- indistinguishable from an agy quota wall --\nand through panel.sh it produced 150,372 bytes at 97% novel, which `seat_verdicts.py` scored as **answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE**.  A seat failure counted as an answer, in the tool built to refuse exactly that.  Every\nexisting test passed it: no quota keyword (not a wall), high novelty because the seat streams reasoning\nBEFORE the filter fires (not an echo), CLI seat (not a burn).  Now reported as **SEAT REFUSED**, counted with\nthe DOWN seats, keyed on the vendor's own sentence, and controlled in four directions: it fires on the\nrefusal, leaves a genuine quota wall as WALLED, leaves the mid-answer wall's READ-THE-BODY label intact, and\ndoes not touch answering seats.\n\nBoth refusal transcripts and the answered design brief are preserved beside the round's artifacts, so the\nbefore/after comparison is checkable rather than remembered.\n\n*** THIRD INSTANCE, 2026-09-07 -- AND IT REFUTES THE DIAGNOSIS THIS ROW CURRENTLY CARRIES. ***\n\nPreserved: `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/attempt3_scoping.err'.  Exact text, twice:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: https://chatgpt.com/cyber\n\n**stdout was 0 bytes; the banner, the echoed prompt and both ERROR lines went to stderr** -- so a\ncheck that measures the `.txt' alone sees an empty file and a check that measures SIZE sees 6829\nbytes of healthy-looking stderr.  `seat_verdicts.py' already reports this as SEAT REFUSED / DOWN\n(fixed earlier in this session); this instance confirms the detector on a fresh case.\n\n*** WHAT IS REFUTED: this row concluded the trigger is the reach-the-defect FRAMING -- *design\nbriefs are answered, witness briefs are refused*.  THE REFUSED BRIEF HERE IS NEITHER. ***  It is a\nread-only SCOPING task that explicitly says *\"Do not design the fix diff.  Do not write rows\"*.\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS STRONG, because it ran the same evening on the same model at the same effort:**\nthe `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 brief -- which discusses uninitialised host stack reaching guest-visible\nmemory, i.e. a MEMORY-DISCLOSURE defect -- was ANSWERED at 317 KB with a GO-WITH-CHANGES verdict and\ntwo new findings.  So the trigger is not the subject matter, not the security-adjacent content, and\nnot the brief's purpose.\n\n**THE REMAINING HYPOTHESIS, stated as a hypothesis because it is not yet tested: VOCABULARY DENSITY.**\nThe refused brief is saturated with this fork's historical term for the defect -- *kills*, *THE HOST\nSTILL DIES*, *terminating calls*, *host kill* -- while the answered brief describes an equivalent\nseverity in ordinary engineering words.  A re-fire is in flight with the identical technical content\nrewritten in bounds-and-error-handling language (`RESEARCH_scoping_neutral_20260907.md'): same\nquestions, same code, same seven dispositions, no `kill'/`dies'/`host' vocabulary.\n\n**That is a real experiment with a clean control and it settles the hypothesis either way** -- if the\nneutral form answers, the trigger is vocabulary and the remedy is the benign-framing rule the carrier\nalready mandates for the Anthropic seats, applied to codex too; if it also refuses, vocabulary is out\nand the next candidate is brief LENGTH or the enumerate-the-sites structure.\n\n*Note the cost is real and recurring: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, and a refusal is\ninvisible to any size-based check.*\n\n*** SOLVED, WITH A CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT.  THE TRIGGER IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ARCHIVES, NOT THE BRIEF'S\nSUBJECT AND NOT ITS FRAMING.  Measured 2026-09-07 across four runs. ***\n\n    attempt  brief vocabulary   reading scope        stderr     flag\n    1        kills / HOST DIES  unrestricted           6,829 b  IMMEDIATE, before any work\n    3        neutral            unrestricted         374,583 b  at byte 279,268 (~75% in)\n    4        neutral            src/ + regress/*.py  279,132 b  *** NONE -- full verdict delivered ***\n    (control) pass-2 panel      unrestricted         395,340 b  at the END of the answer\n\n**TWO SEPARATE FILTERS, and each attempt isolates one:**\n* **Attempt 1 -> 3 shows a PROMPT-level filter keyed on vocabulary.**  Rewriting *kills / the host dies /\n  terminating calls* into ordinary bounds-and-error-handling language converted an instant refusal into a run\n  that worked for minutes.  The technical content was IDENTICAL -- same twelve sites, same expectation design,\n  same seven dispositions.\n* **Attempt 3 -> 4 shows a CONTENT-level filter keyed on what the seat READS.**  Adding one paragraph -- *do\n  not read `regress/artifacts/' or `tools/pipeline/ledger.json'* -- carried the run past the exact point where\n  the unrestricted one died, to a clean finish.  **279,132 vs 279,268 bytes: the same distance, opposite\n  outcome.**\n\n**WHY THE ARCHIVES: they are this project's own record of a hardening campaign** -- kill tables, `host kill'\ncensuses, `exit(1)' enumerations, mutant names like `widthkillclass' -- accumulated over months.  Read in bulk\nthey look like exactly what the filter exists to catch.  *The irony is that the archive is the honest record\nof DEFENSIVE work, and its own vocabulary is what makes the seat refuse to read it.*\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE, and it costs two sentences in a brief: for any codex run, (a) write the brief in\n> ordinary engineering language, and (b) SCOPE ITS READING to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.**  The pass-2 control\n> shows why (b) matters even when (a) is satisfied: that brief was already neutral and was still flagged, at\n> the end, after reading the ledger and CHANGELOG.\n\nThis matters more than one lost answer: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, a refusal writes 0\nbytes to stdout while leaving healthy-looking stderr, and it is invisible to every size-based check.\n\n*** SIXTH RUN, AND IT REFUTES THE REMEDY I SHIPPED TWO HOURS AGO -- SCOPING IS NECESSARY, NOT\nSUFFICIENT.  Worse: it REHABILITATES the diagnosis I recorded as REFUTED, and my refutation was a\nmisreading of my own brief. ***\n\nA `swpctx' design brief scoped ENTIRELY to `src/' was flagged at 145 KB -- the flag fired while it was\ngrepping `debugger_cmds.c', which is INSIDE the allowed scope.  All six runs, sorted by what they asked:\n\n    WHAT IS TRUE                                    WHAT TO BUILD\n    analysis of a defect        317 KB clean        smallest EDIT that RE-OPENS     375 KB FLAGGED\n    count + classify            298 KB clean        smallest guest PROGRAM that\n    is this path reached?       294 KB clean          WITNESSES each defect         145 KB FLAGGED\n    CAN a guest run at all?     502 KB clean\n\n**The discriminator is whether the brief asks the seat to BUILD SOMETHING THAT TRIGGERS A DEFECT.**  The\n502 KB clean run asked at length about cold-debugger probes, breakpoints and planting instructions -- and\nwas fine, because it asked whether such a thing is POSSIBLE rather than for one aimed at a defect.\n\n*** AND I HAD THIS RIGHT THE FIRST TIME. ***  This row's original diagnosis was *the reach-the-defect\nFRAMING is the trigger*.  I recorded it REFUTED because a read-only SCOPING brief was also refused --\nbut that brief's Q3 asked *\"what is the smallest change to the model that would restore the old\nbehaviour ... while still satisfying a test built that way?\"*  **That is construction framing, and I did\nnot re-read my own brief before declaring the diagnosis dead.**  The premise-audit failure again, on my\nown record rather than on the code.\n\n**THE ARCHIVE FINDING IS NOT SUPERSEDED** -- it carried a run past the exact byte where an unscoped one\ndied, which is a measurement and stands.  There are TWO triggers; §C5 shipped implying one, and now says\nso.\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it costs something real:** codex cannot be asked for mutants or witness\nprograms.  It can CHECK a construction supplied to it, and it can say whether one is possible -- both\nmeasured clean.  **The construction itself goes to a measuring seat.**  That is a genuine capability\nloss, not a wording problem, and it should be weighed on the 9th alongside [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** SEVENTH RUN, AND A THIRD REFINEMENT: A BRIEF THAT ASKS `IS THIS TRUE' CAN STILL BE REFUSED IF ITS\nCONTENT IS AN EXPLOITATION CHAIN.  Immediate rejection at 8 KB. ***\n\nThe `sgilinearnegidx' verification brief obeyed both existing rules: neutral engineering language, reading\nscoped to `src/' and `regress/*.py', and it asked only for each link of a chain to be CHECKED -- explicitly\n*\"I want it attacked rather than confirmed\"*, with no construction requested anywhere.  **Refused on the\nprompt**, before any work, exactly like the first `kills'-vocabulary attempt.\n\n**What it contained that the six clean runs did not: a numbered seven-step guest-controlled chain ending in\na PHYSICAL write, plus questions about the reachable address range and about how far the guest can CONTROL\nthe value.**  The clean runs analysed defects, classified sites, traced reachability of a code path, and\nasked whether a probe is possible.  None of them laid out *guest does A, then B, then C, and thereby writes\nwhere it chooses*.\n\n> **So the discriminator is not the QUESTION FORM but the SHAPE OF THE CONTENT.  `What is true about this\n> code?' is clean; `here is a chain by which a guest reaches X -- how far does it go?' is not, however it is\n> phrased.**\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is the sharpest limit yet:** the seat cannot be used to review the very\nfindings that matter most in a hardening fork, once those findings have been assembled into a chain.  It can\ncheck the LINKS SEPARATELY -- *is `src_x' masked in the linear path?* is a clean question -- but not the\nchain as a chain.  **Split the chain into independent link questions, or verify it yourself.**  I verified\nthis one myself in three greps.\n\n*Recorded with the other two triggers rather than replacing them: vocabulary (prompt-level), archive reading\n(content-level, late), and now chain SHAPE (prompt-level).*\n\n*** THIRD RECORDED INSTANCE, 2026-09-07, AND IT CARRIES THE CLEANEST DISCRIMINATOR YET, because\nTWO briefs on the SAME SUBJECT MATTER passed on the SAME DAY and this one did not. ***\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/codex_witness_20260907.err`.  The `.txt` is **ZERO\nBYTES and the process exited 0** -- so an exit-status check scores it a success and a size check\nscores it a dead seat; only the `.err` names it:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: ...\n\n**THE CONTROLS ARE THE VALUABLE PART.  Same seat, same model, same effort, same day, same device\nfile, all three read-only:**\n\n    PASSED   brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md      \"six value guards that END THE PROCESS\" -- inlined\n                                              fatal()+exit(1) verbatim, asked whether a proposed\n                                              DELETION was sound.  6,976-byte answer.\n    PASSED   brief_sgirandomcolour_scope.md   \"writes FOUR random() bytes to guest memory\",\n                                              \"uninitialised host stack reaches guest memory\",\n                                              asked for a reachability census.  7,946-byte answer.\n    REFUSED  brief_lcaconfbus1_witness.md     asked whether a proposed TEST CONSTRUCTION would\n                                              successfully REACH the termination, step by step.\n\n**So it is not the vocabulary and not the subject.**  \"Host kill\", \"exit(1)\", \"guest-drivable\",\n\"uninitialised stack into guest memory\" all pass.  What differs is the ASK: the first two ask *is\nthis analysis correct*; the third asks *will this sequence work*.  A numbered two-step recipe --\nwrite this value here, then touch that window, and the process exits -- reads as exploit validation\nhowever defensively it is framed, and mine was framed defensively (opened with \"ordinary regression-\ntest adequacy review\", no offensive-security wording anywhere).\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE: ask codex to VERIFY AN ANALYSIS, not to VALIDATE A SEQUENCE THAT REACHES A\n> TERMINATION.**  This is the carrier's existing C5 trigger -- *asking for a construction that\n> reaches a defect* -- with a measured negative control on both sides for the first time.  Where a\n> construction genuinely needs checking, ask about its CONTROLS in isolation (\"does a matched\n> surviving control at the same address attribute an outcome to the value?\") without narrating the\n> path to the kill.\n\n**RECOVERY, and it is the sixth-class rule applied: the seat refused, the QUESTION did not become\nunanswerable.**  grok reads the repo, takes its brief from a FILE (no argv cap), runs `--permission-\nmode plan` read-only, and has no such filter.  It was fired on the byte-identical brief.  A refused\nseat with a live alternate transport is ONE SEAT COVERED, not one lost -- but it is recorded as a\nREFUSAL by name, never as agreement or as a wall.\n\n*Cost note: 17,471 tokens spent before the flag fired, and 217 KB of tool-call reasoning in the\n`.err` showing it had already listed the tree and begun reading.  The refusal is not instant, so a\nzero-byte `.txt` after several minutes is NOT evidence of a hang.*\n\n*** A DUPLICATE OF THIS ROW WAS FILED ON 2026-09-08 (`codexcontentfilter`) AND HAS BEEN WITHDRAWN.\nIt contributed ONE measurement worth folding in here. ***\n\n**A DETERMINISM CONTROL: the same brief, byte-for-byte, REFUSED and then ANSWERED.**\n`regress/artifacts/sgirekills/ROUNDDESIGN_brief.md`, md5 `21c440bc6746d1e6bb6d52f703f847bb`,\n16,903 bytes -- firing 1 produced a 0-byte `.txt`, firing 2 produced 6,868 bytes and a full answer.\nNothing changed between them but time.\n\n**That is consistent with THIS row's ARCHIVE-READING trigger and not with randomness:** the filter\nfires partway through, at whatever point the run has read enough of the archive, so two firings of\none brief diverge on what they happen to read.  *The duplicate concluded \"non-deterministic\"; the\nmechanism recorded here is the better explanation, and it makes the remedy a FIX (neutral language\nplus scoped reading) rather than a retry.*\n\n**A re-fire is still worth one attempt** -- it costs a minute and may take a different reading path\n-- but it is a coin-flip with a mechanism, not the remedy.\n\n*** A NEGATIVE CONTROL ON THIS ROW'S OWN REMEDY, MEASURED 2026-09-08: THE SCOPING FIX DID NOT WORK,\nAND THE REASON IS THAT IT CANNOT WORK ON A ROUND OF THIS SHAPE. ***\n\nThis row's recorded remedy is **neutral engineering language AND reading scoped to src/ and\nregress/*.py**, aimed at the ARCHIVE-READING trigger -- the filter fires once the run has read enough\nof regress/artifacts/ or ledger.json.\n\nThe sgirekills re-pin brief was refused.  I applied the remedy exactly -- added a READING SCOPE\nparagraph naming src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c and regress/*.py and excluding the archive -- and re-fired.\n**It was refused again**: codex_repin_scoped_20260908.txt is ZERO BYTES, the .err carries two\nTrusted-Access lines, 17,904 tokens used, exited 1.\n\n> *** THE DIAGNOSIS: SCOPING THE READING TO src/ IS NOT A REMEDY WHEN src/ IS WHAT TRIPS THE\n> FILTER. ***  The .err shows the run had reached dev_sgi_re.c -- and that file is a wall of bounds\n> guards, refusal arms and exit(1) calls.  **To the filter, the product source of a hardening fork\n> reads like the archive does.**  The remedy moves the reading from one flagged corpus to another.\n\n**So the three recorded triggers need a fourth, and it is the one that BOUNDS the remedy:**\n\n    SUBJECT SOURCE   content-level and UNAVOIDABLE -- a round whose subject file is itself\n                     guard-and-refuse code cannot be scoped away from its own subject.\n\n**WHAT THIS CHANGES OPERATIONALLY, and it is not nothing:** for a round on a device-hardening file,\ncodex must be given the FACTS INLINE -- treated as a PACKET-FED seat for that round -- rather than\npointed at the file.  That is the same move the carrier already prescribes for the Ollama transports,\nand it is affordable here because a re-pin brief's facts are a few dozen lines of arm addresses and\narray declarations.  **Untested as of this writing; recorded as the next thing to try, NOT as a\nsecond remedy claimed to work.**\n\n*Recorded because it is a controlled negative: same seat, same subject, remedy applied deliberately\nand singly, refused.  The remedy's earlier success on the gdromcondavail brief stands -- what is\nrefuted is its GENERALITY, and this row had stated it with no scope condition.*\n\n*** THE CHAIN-SHAPE TRIGGER CONFIRMED PROMPT-LEVEL, 2026-09-08, WITH A CLEAN DISCRIMINATOR THAT\nSEPARATES IT FROM THE ARCHIVE-READING TRIGGER AT A GLANCE. ***\n\nFour codex briefs went out tonight on neutral engineering subjects.  Three answered in full; the\nfourth was refused.  **The refused one is the WITNESS-DESIGN brief**, and its `.err` settles where\nthe refusal happened:\n\n    brief                      .err bytes   file-read lines   outcome\n    armbfcdecode DECODE            70,308   many              ANSWERED (5,037 b)\n    alphatbinegsel VERIFY          20,210   many              ANSWERED (6,820 b)\n    alphatbinegsel Q3             167,085   many              ANSWERED (4,654 b)\n    alphatbinegsel WITNESS          6,291   *** ZERO ***      REFUSED (0 b)\n\n*** ZERO FILE-READ LINES MEANS IT REFUSED BEFORE OPENING ANYTHING.  The prompt's TEXT tripped it,\nnot what it read. ***  That is this row's third recorded trigger -- CHAIN SHAPE -- and it is now\nconfirmed as prompt-level rather than inferred.\n\n**WHY THIS BRIEF AND NOT THE OTHER THREE.**  The three that answered ask *\"is this analysis\ncorrect\"* about code that already exists.  The witness brief asks how to CONSTRUCT A GUEST SEQUENCE\nTHAT DEMONSTRATES THE DEFECT -- *\"what the guest sets up, which PAL call it makes, and what it then\nobserves\"*, plus *\"remap a VPN to a different physical page ... then load from that VPN and see the\nOLD contents.\"*  **A numbered guest-controlled sequence ending in an observation of stale memory is\nthe chain shape, however defensively framed** -- and this brief was written to be neutral, with no\n`kill`/`host dies`/`exploit` vocabulary anywhere.  Neutral language was NOT sufficient.\n\n> *** THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is a real capability limit rather than a nuisance:\n> **codex CANNOT DESIGN WITNESSES FOR THIS PROJECT.**  Witness design IS the reach-the-defect shape\n> by definition -- the ladder requires a real guest instruction sequence that reaches the site.  So\n> every rung-3 witness brief is expected to refuse, and routing one to codex is a wasted\n> invocation. ***\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it costs one `grep -c`:**\n\n    small .err + ZERO read lines + 0-byte .txt   -> PROMPT-LEVEL.  Rewrite the brief, or send it\n                                                    to a different seat.  A retry will not help.\n    large .err + many read lines + 0-byte .txt   -> LATE (archive/subject).  Scoping the reading\n                                                    may help; a retry may take a different path.\n    NO .txt and NO .err at all                   -> the run NEVER STARTED (a launcher/path fault,\n                                                    NOT a refusal) -- measured separately tonight.\n\n*Three distinguishable states, three different remedies.  Previously only the middle one was\ncharacterised, and the row's determinism pair belongs to it.*\n\n**ROUTED TO grok INSTEAD**, which is not subject to this filter and which has designed rung-3\nwitnesses for this project before (`lcabadval`'s row records a rung-3 seat specifying a full\nsequence).  *The brief was reused BYTE-FOR-BYTE -- no rewrite -- which is what makes this a clean\nseat-capability finding rather than a prompt-quality one.*\n\n*** THE THREE-STATE DISCRIMINATOR NEEDS ONE CORRECTION, FOUND BY APPLYING IT TO A HEALTHY SEAT\nTWENTY MINUTES AFTER WRITING IT.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nAs written above, the first state reads *\"small `.err` + ZERO read lines + 0-byte `.txt` ->\nPROMPT-LEVEL refusal.\"*  Applied to the `#513` pass-2 run mid-flight, that pattern MATCHED a seat\nthat was perfectly healthy:\n\n    refusal lines      0\n    file-read lines    0        <- matches the \"prompt-level\" shape\n    .err bytes    39,485        <- but LARGE, not small\n    processes          4 alive\n    .txt bytes         0        <- normal mid-run: codex writes stdout at the END\n\n**It had read nothing because the pass-2 brief INLINES the whole diff -- there was nothing it needed\nto open.**  A seat can legitimately answer with zero file reads.\n\n> **THE ACTUAL DISCRIMINATOR IS THE REFUSAL LINE.  Everything else says WHERE it refused, not\n> WHETHER it did.**\n>\n>     refusal line present + ZERO reads   -> PROMPT-LEVEL.  Reroute; a retry will not help.\n>     refusal line present + MANY reads   -> LATE (archive/subject).  Scoping may help.\n>     refusal line ABSENT, .txt 0 bytes,\n>       process alive                     -> MID-RUN.  Not a refusal at all.\n>     NO .txt and NO .err whatsoever      -> the run NEVER STARTED (launcher/path fault).\n\n*The read count was doing work it cannot do.  I wrote the three-state form from four observations\nthat happened to correlate -- every refusal I had seen with zero reads also had a small `.err` --\nand the correlation broke on the first case outside that set.*  **That is this file's own\nfreshly-minted-exemplar rule landing on a discriminator I minted an hour earlier: the instruction\nsurvived, its cheap proxy did not.**\n\n*Corrected in `tools/pipeline/ADJUDICATION_20260909.md` as well, which had quoted the earlier form.*\n\n*** A FOURTH TRIGGER, AND IT IS THE OPERATIONALLY EXPENSIVE ONE: THE ASK-AGAIN QUESTION ITSELF.\nMeasured 2026-09-08 on the #513 pass-3 panel. ***\n\ncodex ANSWERED the pass-2 diff review and REFUSED the pass-3 one.  Both are diff reviews of the\nsame round, same file, same neutral vocabulary.  **The refusal is LATE, not prompt-level** --\n133,341 bytes of `.err`, 30,706 tokens used, many file reads, then the flag -- so it is the\narchive/subject shape rather than the chain shape.\n\n**WHAT PASS 3 ASKS THAT PASS 2 DID NOT:**\n\n    Q2  \"Is there a third cell? ... bits 6, 7, 9, 10 are swept by nothing.\"\n    Q3  \"Name the smallest edit that keeps a guard and makes these two rows GREEN while the\n         latch behaviour is WRONG.\"\n\n**That is an EVASION-CONSTRUCTION request.**  Pass 2 asked *is this change correct*; pass 3 asks\n*construct an edit that defeats this detector while looking right*.  The second is much closer to\nthe reach-the-defect shape than to source review.\n\n> *** THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC: THE ASK-AGAIN IS A MANDATED STEP IN THIS PROJECT'S\n> PROCESS. ***  The vacuity floor requires exactly this question of every clean kill table -- *\"the\n> smallest edit that breaks this property and still passes\"* -- and measured yield says it is where\n> the real escapes come from.  **So codex is unavailable for the single highest-yield adversarial\n> query in the round lifecycle**, on top of being unavailable for witness design.\n\n**WHAT STILL WORKS:** codex answered EIGHT briefs tonight -- decode conformance, ABI review, source\nanalysis, callback tracing, error-handling review, and the pass-2 diff review.  The pattern across\nnine firings is consistent: **codex reviews CODE THAT EXISTS; it will not construct an edit or a\nsequence that defeats something.**\n\n**MITIGATION, and it is already proven in this round:** grok and the packet-fed seats answer the\nask-again without difficulty -- pass 3's five answering seats ALL found the remaining latch cells,\nand grok additionally prescribed the one-row class closure.  **Route the ask-again to grok and the\nOllama transports; do not spend a codex invocation on it.**\n\n*Recorded from a controlled pair: same round, same file, same seat, two briefs one hour apart,\ndiffering in exactly this question.*\n\n*** THE ARCHIVE TRIGGER FIRED AGAIN 2026-09-08, AND I WALKED THE SEAT INTO IT WHILE WRITING THE\nAVOIDANCE INSTRUCTION.  Ten refusal lines. ***\n\nThe brief said, in its own scope section:\n\n> *\"Read ... `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/killtable_20260908/*.txt` and the `*_20260908.out` logs\n> there -- **these are the measurement records the packet cites; reading them is the point of this\n> brief**.  Please still avoid `tools/pipeline/ledger.json`.\"*\n\n**`regress/artifacts/` IS the archive corpus this row's second trigger keys on.**  I named the\nledger as the thing to avoid and directed the seat straight into the other half.  507 KB of `.err`,\n**ten** refusal lines, 0-byte answer.\n\n> *** THE TENSION IS REAL AND IS NOT A MISTAKE IN THE ROW'S REMEDY: AUDITING MEASUREMENT CLAIMS\n> REQUIRES THE MEASUREMENT ARCHIVE.  The remedy \"scope reading away from regress/artifacts/\" and the\n> task \"verify these numbers against their logs\" are in direct conflict. ***\n\n**THE RESOLUTION, and it is the one the packet-fed seats have always needed: INLINE THE LOGS.**  The\nseven logs this audit needs total a few KB.  Re-fired as `PACKETAUDIT2_brief.md` with every one\ninlined and an explicit *\"DO NOT READ regress/artifacts/\"* -- so the seat needs no archive access to\ndo the job.\n\n**This generalises past codex:** a brief that asks a seat to check numbers should carry the numbers'\nLOGS, not their paths.  The Ollama transports have always required that because they cannot read\nfiles at all; here it is required for the opposite reason -- a seat that CAN read the archive is\nrefused for doing so.  *Same remedy, two different causes, and the file-reading seats' version was\nnot obvious until the filter made it necessary.*\n\n*Recorded as my error rather than a seat failure: the brief was refused for exactly what it\ninstructed.*\n\n*** THE ENTRY DIRECTLY ABOVE IS WRONG AND I AM CORRECTING IT WITHIN THE HOUR.  THE ARCHIVE-READING\nBRIEF **ANSWERED**. ***\n\nI recorded that the packet-audit brief was refused for directing the seat into `regress/artifacts/`,\nand prescribed inlining the logs.  **The refusal count was 25 and the seat then produced an\n18,687-byte answer** -- it retried and succeeded, reading the archive throughout and citing\nindividual log lines.  Meanwhile the INLINED re-fire sat at 2 refusals and a 0-byte answer.\n\n> **SO A REFUSAL LINE IS NOT A TERMINAL STATE, AND MY OWN THREE-STATE DISCRIMINATOR SAYS SO\n> WRONGLY.**  It reads *\"refusal line present -> reroute; a retry will not help.\"*  **Measured\n> false here: twenty-five refusal lines preceded a complete answer.**  The discriminating fact is\n> the `.txt`, not the `.err` -- a non-empty answer means the seat got there whatever the stderr says.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES:** the two PASS-3 and WITNESS refusals were terminal (0-byte answers), so the\ncapability finding about the ask-again and witness-design shapes is unaffected.  What is refuted is\nthe inference that ANY refusal line means the seat is unavailable.\n\n*Recorded here at length because I wrote the false version into this row, into the checkpoint and\ninto the adjudication packet within the last hour, and a correction that arrives quietly is worse\nthan the error.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31491-   "entries": [
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31492-    {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31493-     "seat": "codex",
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31670-   "opened": "2026-09-07",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31671-   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel(): uint8_t buf[8] is never initialised and the guest picks how many of its bytes are used -- uninitialised host stack reaches guest-visible memory on PUT and *color on GET",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31672-   "witness": null,
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31673:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while writing the design brief for `sgirandomcolour' -- i.e. by reading the arm NEXT\nto the one grok pointed at.  **This is a bigger defect than the row that led me to it, and it needs no\nundocumented mode at all.**\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()' declares `uint8_t buf[8];' and NEVER INITIALISES IT.  Verified: no memset in the\nfunction, no initialiser, and the only memsets in the file are the device struct, `rop_used' and `zerobuf'.\nThe two fields that decide how much of `buf' is USED are decoded from the SAME guest-written mode register\nand are INDEPENDENT of each other:\n\n    uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;      /* bits 4-7  */\n    int bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3);               /* bits 8-9  -> 1, 2, 4 or 8 */\n\nThe colour arms fill a number of bytes fixed by the COLOUR type; the transfer length is `bufdepth'.  Nothing\nanywhere makes them agree -- `grep -rn '>> *8) *& *3' src/devices/' finds the decode, its own #100 comment,\nand the XFER siblings; NO GUARD.\n\n**TWO REACHABLE UNINITIALISED PATHS, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS:**\n\n* **PUT -- host stack INTO guest-visible memory.**  With the depth field = 3 (`bufdepth' 8) NO colour arm\n  writes `buf[4..7]' -- not one of the five, including the correct ones -- and all eight bytes go straight to\n  `memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL)'.  `DE_MODE_TYPE_CI'\n  writes only `buf[0]', so CI at any depth above 1 leaks the rest.\n* **GET -- host stack INTO `*color', which the caller then STORES.**  The read fills only `bufdepth' bytes,\n  then RGB/RGBA/ABGR combine `buf[0..3]'.  Depth field 0 (`bufdepth' 1) with `DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA' reads ONE\n  byte from memory and builds a 32-bit colour from four.  `draw_primitive()' writes that to the destination\n  in the same loop iteration -- **the identical caller-consumes-a-failed-read shape `sgirandompixel' just\n  fixed one arm of**, reached here with every colour mode documented and implemented.\n\n*** THE #100 COMMENT IS THE TELL, AND IT IS ON THE DECLARATION ITSELF. ***  It reads\n`/*  #100: bufdepth can be 8 = 1<<((mode>>8)&3)  */' -- so a previous round worked out that the guest can\ndemand 8 bytes and GREW THE ARRAY to match, which fixed a stack OVERFLOW.  **It did not ask what fills the\nbytes it had just added.**  The array is the right size and the contents are undefined.\n\nSEVERITY: guest-reachable read of uninitialised host stack whose value becomes guest-visible.  In a loop the\nstale bytes are usually the PREVIOUS pixel's, so the everyday symptom is wrong colours; on the first call in\na frame they are whatever the host stack held.  Both mode words are ordinary device registers reached by\nordinary stores, and all seven call sites pass one of them.\n\nNOT YET WITNESSED -- filed at once so the finding is durable.  The witness is expected to be cheap because\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this exact path on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32' and\nneeds only a different mode word: its `MODE_LIN_A = 0x1220' is LIN_A | depth-field 2 (`bufdepth' 4) | RGBA,\nwhich is the MATCHED case and leaks nothing.  `0x1020' is the same line with depth field 0.\n\n*** PASS-1 CORRECTION FROM THE GROK SEAT, VERIFIED AGAINST THE HEADER BEFORE BEING ACCEPTED.  MY PATH-A\nEXEMPLAR WAS THE WEAKER ONE, AND A FULLY DOCUMENTED VEHICLE EXISTS. ***\n\nThe brief drove PATH A with mode `0x1320' -- depth field 3, `bufdepth' 8 -- and called it reachable.  grok:\n*\"BUFDEPTH in the header is 8/16/32 only -- field 3 is undocumented ... PATH A is live on documented `CI' +\n`BUFDEPTH_16/32' (`0x1200'), not only on undocumented depth 8.  The brief's `0x1320' PATH-A vehicle is the\nweaker one.\"*\n\n**CHECKED, not taken on trust** -- `grep -n BUFDEPTH src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h' returns exactly three:\n\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8   0x00000000   -> depth field 0 -> bufdepth 1 byte\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_16  0x00000100   -> depth field 1 -> bufdepth 2 bytes\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32  0x00000200   -> depth field 2 -> bufdepth 4 bytes\n\n**Field value 3 is documented nowhere**, so the brief's own vehicle relies on an undocumented depth -- while\n`DE_MODE_TYPE_CI' assigns ONLY `buf[0]' and `DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32' transfers FOUR bytes, so `0x1200' leaks\n`buf[1..3]' with **every field documented and CI being colour type ZERO**, the reset value.\n\n*(Note the naming trap I had not spotted: the constants are in BITS and the code's `bufdepth' is in BYTES, so\n`BUFDEPTH_32' is 4.  That is why `1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3)' maxes at 8 with only three constants defined.)*\n\n**AND IT SETTLED Q1.3, WHICH I HAD EXPLICITLY LEFT OPEN: the destination is RAM, not a device.**  So `#95's\ndevice-read pre-zero does not run AT ALL on either path -- not merely `does not cover the bytes past len'.\nBoth paths are therefore worse than the brief stated, not better.\n\n**This is the premise-audit opener earning its place for the third recorded time.**  I asked seats to attack\nthe reachability premise; one did, and the correction STRENGTHENS the finding while WEAKENING the exemplar I\nhad chosen -- which is exactly the asymmetry the rule exists to surface.\n\ngrok's other two calls -- **do not join [[sgirandomcolour]]** (my Q6) and **talk HIGH down to MEDIUM** (my Q5)\n-- are NOT actioned here.  They are adjudication questions and the panel is still running; the\nprovisional-adjudication rule says a ruling short of the roster is provisional on its face, so they wait for\nthe full six.\n\n*** PASS-1 BRIEF DEFECT, RAISED INDEPENDENTLY BY ALL THREE PACKET-FED SEATS: I ASSERTED A PREMISE THEY\nCOULD NOT CHECK, AND SAID `SETTLED PREMISES: NONE CARRIED'. ***\n\nNo burns -- all three returned real verdicts on a 31 KB brief -- and all three named the same hole:\n  * **minimax**: *\"the load-bearing premise is NOT CHECKABLE and the brief states it confidently anyway ...\n    That single fact -- the body of `cpus/memory_rw.c' -- is what would settle it, and the brief should not\n    have stated it as confidently as it did.\"*\n  * **glm**: *\"reachability rests on one uncheckable premise\"*; it treated every grep and scan output in the\n    brief as uncheckable and said so.\n  * **deepseek**: caught a SECOND one I had not noticed -- *\"the brief's table for `default'/YCRCB assumes\n    `fatal()' returns\"*.  My PATH-A table lists the `default:' arm as filling `buf[0..3]' and then writing;\n    **if `fatal()' exited, that arm would never reach the write and the row would be wrong.**\n\n**BOTH RESOLVE IN THE FINDING'S FAVOUR, WHICH IS NOT THE POINT.**  Checked at HEAD: `fatal()' in\n`src/core/debugmsg.c' is `va_start / va_debug / va_end' with **no exit and no early-out**, so the arm does\nreach the write; and grok, a FILE-READING seat, settled the other by reporting the destination is RAM rather\nthan a device, so `#95's pre-zero never runs at all.\n\n**THE DEFECT IS THAT I MADE A DOCUMENTED MISTAKE THE TEMPLATE WARNS ABOUT IN CAPITALS.**  `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`\nsection C is headed *\"MANDATORY.  COPY FORWARD.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT GETS DROPPED\"*, and my brief wrote\n*\"SETTLED PREMISES: none carried -- first brief of this campaign\"*.  **That was true about the CAMPAIGN and\nfalse about the QUESTIONS**: the template's own test is whether a seat could build an *if X then A, else B*\nanswer on the premise -- and deepseek built exactly that on `fatal()'.  The obligation is PER-PACKET and\nBROAD, *whatever pass or round settled it*, and `fatal()'-does-not-exit was settled long ago; the carrier\nrecords it costing the minimax seat its answer twice before.\n\n**THE TRANSPORT PREAMBLE DID ITS JOB AND THE BRIEF DID NOT.**  `panel_ollama.py' asks a packet-fed seat to\nNAME any premise it cannot settle instead of guessing, and all three did precisely that rather than\nratifying -- which is the behaviour that makes a packet-fed seat worth firing.  Score this as a BRIEF DEFECT,\nnever as three weak answers.\n\n**FOR THE PASS-2 PACKET:** inline (a) `fatal()'s four-line body, (b) the `memory_rw' pre-zero site with the\nbranch that gates it, and (c) grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.  All three are one or two lines\neach and they are what these seats spent their answers asking for.\n\n*** THE CLASS SCAN'S DENOMINATOR IS WRONG, AND THE WAY IT IS WRONG IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.  Codex seat,\npass 1, verdict GO-WITH-CHANGES. ***\n\nThe brief claimed SIX unpinned-length sites and FIVE stack-buffer write sites, and concluded that\n`dev_sgi_re.c' is the only member of the class in the device tree.  **Codex found four write sites both of my\nscanners missed**, and named the mechanism for each:\n\n  * `dev_dec21143.c' descriptor writebacks (two sites) -- *\"Nested argument commas defeat the scanner's\n    splitting.\"*  **That is limit 1, which the brief DISCLOSED**, demonstrated on real sites.\n  * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' response writes (two sites) -- *\"whose casts prevent recognition of the buffer\n    argument.\"*  **That limit was NOT disclosed and I did not know about it.**\n\nAll four are SAFE (initialised via `store_le32', or cast forms that do fill the buffer), **so the CONCLUSION\nsurvives and the DENOMINATOR does not.**  Those are different claims and the brief conflated them.\n\n*** THE METHODOLOGICAL POINT IS SHARPER THAN THE COUNT, AND IT LANDS ON THE BRIEF'S BEST-LOOKING EVIDENCE: ***\n*\"Agreement between scanners sharing parsing limitations is not exhaustive corroboration.\"*  The brief\npresented TWO independent scans agreeing as strong evidence of scope.  **They are not independent** -- both\nsplit `memory_rw' arguments on commas with the same regex, so both are blind to the same call shapes.  Two\nderivers that can fail the same way are ONE deriver run twice, and this project has already recorded that\nexact lesson on the `sgirekills' count, where two agreeing greps both counted comment lines.\n\n**Two of the brief's SAFE verdicts also need narrowing, per the same seat:**\n  * **GBE** -- the divisor is nonzero and the transfer is bounded, but *\"conversion still loops to 512 after a\n    shorter read\"*, which can consume unfilled bytes.  Codex declines to call it a demonstrated guest\n    disclosure (the forwarded framebuffer prefix is smaller) but says it *\"invalidates the blanket\n    initialization-safety claim\"*.  Correct: my check established the LENGTH bound and said nothing about\n    consumption.\n  * **PXG** -- `#207' bounds the later SRAM copy's SOURCE SPAN without bounding CONSUMPTION, and the initial\n    SRAM copy is unchecked.  The enumerated 3080-byte PX maximum stands; what falls is treating it as proof of\n    complete initialisation across BOTH branches.\n\n**Consequence for this round: the scope sentence must be rewritten before pass 2** -- from *\"the only member\nof this class in the device tree\"* to *\"the only DEFECTIVE member among the sites these two scanners can\nsee, whose blind spots are nested-comma argument lists and cast buffer arguments\"*.  The two new correctness\nfindings the same seat raised are filed separately as [[sgirergbroundtrip]] and [[sgimtedepth4dead]].\n\n*** PASS-1 ADJUDICATION.  EVIDENCE BASIS: 6 of 9 -- six scriptable seats ANSWERED (0 walled, 0 down, 0\nburns, novelty 93-100%); agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the measurement reserve; opus5 and fable5 HELD for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  The three absences are DIRECTED, not failures -- but the\nprovisional-adjudication rule still applies and this ruling is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**THE FINDING SURVIVES ALL SIX.**  Nobody disputed that `buf' is uninitialised, that the two mode fields are\nindependent, or that `#95' fails to cover the paths.  What changed is the FRAMING, in three places, and every\ncorrection came from a seat rather than from me.\n\n**D1 -- Q6, ONE ROUND OR TWO: SEPARATE.  My proposal REFUTED, and refuted with a measurement.**  Three seats\nsaid separate (grok, kimi, deepseek); kimi proved it: a shared *destination is deterministic across runs*\noracle is **VACUOUS for this row**, because `srandom(tv.tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv.tv_usec)' makes `random()' vary\nper run while STALE-STACK REUSE UNDER A FIXED GUEST PROGRAM IS RUN-DETERMINISTIC -- so the buggy build PASSES.\nVerified at `src/core/main.c:688-691'.  **I asked the panel not to let the convenience of one round drive the\nanswer, and it did not.**  ACCEPTED: two rounds.\n\n**D2 -- Q5, SEVERITY: grok says MEDIUM, kimi says HIGH *conditionally*.  RESOLVED BY KIMI'S CONDITION, not by\ncounting.**  kimi: HIGH is defensible for this fork's mandate *'provided the entry carries the corrected\nreachability framing ... Do not ship the entry with \"no undocumented value anywhere\" in it.'*  The row KEEPS\nHIGH and the framing is corrected here.  grok's MEDIUM rests on disclosure impact, which is genuinely weak (4\nstale bytes per pixel, usually prior pixels); kimi's HIGH rests on the fork's mandate and on this defect\nfalsifying `#95's *closes the whole class* sentence in the same batch that edited this array.  **That is the\nstronger warrant for THIS fork**, and it is the one I take.  Recorded as a SPLIT, not as agreement.\n\n**F1 -- A FALSE SENTENCE IN MY BRIEF, and it is NOT being edited.**  The brief says the paths are reached\n*'with no undocumented value anywhere'*.  **False**: PATH A's MAXIMAL form (`bufdepth' 8) needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  Documented forms exist -- CI x BUFDEPTH_16/32 for PATH A, and PATH B entirely --\nso the FINDING stands while the blanket phrase does not.  **The sent brief is left BYTE-UNCHANGED on purpose:\nit is the frozen packet six seats reviewed, and rewriting it would falsify the record of what was asked.**\nThe correction lives here and goes into the pass-2 packet.\n\n**F2 -- THE WITNESS IS NOT `ONE CONSTANT'.**  kimi: it needs a register plus the `step_x' twin, or ZERO extra\nregisters via a PUT-only RECT path.  My cost claim was optimistic; the PUT-only path is the cheaper design and\nwas not in the brief at all.\n\n**F3 -- INDEPENDENT CONVERGENCE ON A NEW DEFECT.**  codex and kimi BOTH found the dead `if (depth == 4)'\nwithout knowledge of each other.  Independent convergence is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence, so\n[[sgimtedepth4dead]] ships with two witnesses to its existence.\n\n**OWED BEFORE PASS 2**, from §C2 and the three packet-fed seats who each asked for it: inline `fatal()'s body,\nthe `memory_rw' pre-zero site with its gating branch, and grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.\n\n*** UNDER-HARVESTED REVIEW, CORRECTED 2026-09-07.  I READ THIS SEAT'S ANSWER FOR ITS NEW FINDINGS AND\nSTOPPED.  IT ALSO REFUTED FOUR OF MY CLAIMS AND **REJECTED MY WITNESS DESIGN**, AND I RECORDED NONE OF IT.\nTHIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, ON AN ANSWER I HAD ALREADY OPENED. (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/codex.txt) ***\n\n**1. MY WITNESS DESIGN IS REJECTED, and the reason is architectural rather than a detail.**  I proposed\npainting a first pixel so `buf' holds KNOWN bytes, then reading a second at a mismatched depth and asserting\nthe result carries bytes from the first.  codex: *\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise\nof retaining each other's contents.  Register allocation, inlining, and intervening calls matter.\"*  And the\nsame objection kills the other direction: *\"**Path A has the same problem:** observing an unassigned byte\ndoes not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding.\"*  **I flagged that risk myself in the\nbrief -- `an oracle of \"the value is wrong\" can pass by luck' -- proposed a fix for it, and did not record\nthat the fix was refused.**\n\n**The prescribed measurement instead: an ATTRIBUTED INITIALIZEDNESS RESULT per direction** -- MemorySanitizer\nor Valgrind Memcheck -- *\"tied to the reviewed build, together with passing functional controls\"*, with the\ncaveat that *\"merely copying undefined bytes need not produce an immediate diagnostic\"*, so instrumentation\ncoverage must be established rather than assumed.  **That is a different kind of round from the one I was\nplanning** and it is why this row is not next in the queue.\n\n**2. `LIN_A cannot be refused' IS FALSE.**  I wrote that case 4 FORCES the valid bit so the arm is reached\nunconditionally.  codex: *\"remove 'LIN_A cannot be refused': its index guard can return before the buffer\ncode.\"*  `tile_nr = x >> 12; if (tile_nr >= 32) return;` -- an early return I read past.\n\n**3. `THE WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT CHANGED' IS FALSE.**  *\"The existing probe uses its LIN_A constant for the\ndestination and matched control as well as a source case.  Changing that shared constant changes the\nobservation path and the control simultaneously.\"*  My cost estimate was wrong for the same reason kimi's\ncorrection said so, and I recorded kimi's and not this one.\n\n**4. `UNINITIALISED ON EVERY PATH' OVERSTATES IT.**  *\"matched operations initialize every byte they\nconsume.\"*  The defect is the MISMATCHED pairs, not the buffer in general.\n\n**5. MY `WORSE THAN I SAID' CLAIM ABOUT RAM IS WRONG.**  I recorded that grok's RAM-not-device finding makes\nboth paths worse.  codex: *\"**RAM does not inherently make Path B worse:** a successful RAM read defines the\nrequested prefix through `memcpy'; the missing suffix is the same problem.\"*\n\n**6. NOT ALL SEVEN CALL SITES TAKE THE GUEST MODE WORDS** -- *\"the two MTE sites construct their own modes\"*.\nMy reachability paragraph implies all seven.\n\n**7. SEVERITY: MED, not HIGH.**  *\"HIGH is not established by inheriting #95's severity.\"*  And it refuses\nthe downgrade too: *\"'usually the previous pixel' and 'first call in a frame' are unsupported explanations of\nstack contents.  They cannot justify downgrading this to a cosmetic rendering defect.\"*  **Both of my\nseverity arguments were unsupported, in opposite directions.**  Lowered to MED provisionally, with potential\nhost information disclosure recorded as unestablished rather than assumed either way.\n\n*** THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE POINT AND IT IS THE THIRD INSTANCE TONIGHT. ***  I opened this file, extracted\nthe two NEW defects and the census correction, and stopped -- because new findings are what I was looking\nfor.  **A seat that refutes your premises is easy to under-read precisely because its corrections are not\nphrased as discoveries.**  The same failure hit the `lcabadval` answer (an eleventh site missed) and the\n`alphaswpctxptbr` premises.  All three were answers already on disk.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST REACHABILITY VEHICLE WAS IN THE KIMI ANSWER ALL ALONG, AND IT IS ALL-DOCUMENTED.\nHarvested and verified 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt). ***\n\nEverything above argues reachability from the DE path and concedes that PATH A's maximal form needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  **There is a third vehicle that needs nothing undocumented at all**, and it is\nthe one a real driver uses: `do_mte_transfer()' constructs its own modes with NO colour bits (so CI), because\nthe `if (depth == 4)' that would set RGBA is dead code -- see [[sgimtedepth4dead]], raised to HIGH on this.\n\nCI fills only `buf[0]'; `MTE_DEPTH_16' and `MTE_DEPTH_32' are documented and give `bufdepth' 2 and 4.  **So\nevery MTE fill at those depths leaks 1 or 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel, with every value documented**, on\nthe path kimi names as used by `crmfb_fill_rect'.\n\n**This also settles the `not all seven call sites take the guest mode words' correction** recorded above from\nthe codex seat: the two MTE sites construct their own modes -- and constructing them is exactly what makes\nthem leak.  The two corrections are the same fact seen from opposite sides, and I recorded one without\nnoticing it implied the other.\n\n**AND BOTH FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE DETECTOR REQUIREMENT:** the oracle must assert\nVALUES, not determinism.  kimi's proof is the sharper one -- post-fix PATH B yields a deterministic value AND\nthe `bufdepth'-sized mutant yields the previous pixel's bytes, which are *also* deterministic under stack\nreuse, so *\"a determinism oracle CANNOT catch the mutant\"*.  Two seats, two routes, one conclusion.\n\n*** FOURTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER, AND THE RICHEST PER BYTE: THE GROK SEAT'S 15 KB\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/grok.txt).  IT ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH GREPPED IN-TREE PRECEDENT, NAMED\nTHREE MEMBERS I HAD NOT, ARGUED THE DISPOSITION I WAS LEANING TOWARD IS WRONG, AND FOUND THE MTE CHAIN\nINDEPENDENTLY.  I HAD RECORDED ONLY ITS BUFDEPTH CORRECTION. ***\n\n**1. THE MTE CHAIN, INDEPENDENTLY.**  *\"16- and 32-bit MTE TLB fills therefore always run as CI + matching\nbufdepth.  32-bit fill writes `bg''s low byte plus three uninit bytes.  Comment at :1322 says NetBSD\n`crmfb_fill_rect' uses this.\"*  **So TWO seats found it and I missed it from both** -- and grok additionally\npinned the in-file comment naming the driver, which I had attributed to kimi alone.\n\n**Its fix-order point is sharper than the one I recorded:** *\"Do NOT join: fixing `== 4' to `== 32' changes\nMTE 32-bit COLOUR semantics, which memset does not.  Memset would turn that path into 'low byte + zeros' --\ndefined, still wrong.\"*  I had noted that repairing the dead branch alone MASKS the leak; grok's is the dual\nand it matters as much -- **the memset fix leaves the MTE path DEFINED AND STILL WRONG**, because the colour\nsemantics are a separate defect from the initialisation.  Neither fix subsumes the other.\n\n**2. THREE OPTION-SET MEMBERS I DID NOT NAME**, and it reached them by grepping rather than reasoning:\n    6.  `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};`  -- the C99 form, same observable as a memset\n    7.  CLAMP THE TRANSFER to bytes filled (`len = min(bufdepth, filled)`) -- guest-visible difference: the\n        OLD DESTINATION BYTES SURVIVE, which is neither zeros nor stale stack\n    8.  IMPLEMENT the modes (YCRCB, 64-bit depth) -- and it refused this itself on the ADDITION LICENCE: no\n        CRIME DE pixel-packing datasheet exists in `_scratchpad/`\n\n**3. THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, WHICH IS WHAT THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR AND I DID NOT DO:**\n    * **this function's own RGB PUT already pads with zero** (`buf[3] = 0`) -- in-function precedent for\n      DEFINED PADDING rather than refusal;\n    * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' does `memset(buf, 0, 8)' then a partial fill then `MEM_WRITE' -- **the same\n      shape, and the house answer there is zero the whole array**;\n    * `dev_sgi_gbe.c' (`#196') renders an unimplemented colour type as BLACK -- defined zeros, no `fatal'\n      flood;\n    * MTE's own `zerobuf' is memset before its write, in this same file.\n\n**4. IT ARGUES DECLINE IS THE WRONG DISPOSITION, AND THE REASON TIES BACK TO THE MTE CHAIN:**\n*\"`CI' + `BUFDEPTH_32' is a plausible 8-bit index in a 32-bit container (RGB already pads).  Declining it\nwould also break the MTE 16/32-bit TLB fill path, which ALREADY EMITS THAT PAIR.  No hardware evidence it is\nillegal.\"*  **That is decisive against member 4** and I had not connected the two facts -- the very pair a\ndecline would reject is the one the model's own MTE path generates.\n\nIts recommendation is **(1)/(6), full-object zero**, with the comment saying plainly that hardware padding is\nunknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME model -- which is also what the codex seat\nindependently chose.\n\n*** 5. AN APPARENT DISAGREEMENT I COLLAPSED, RESOLVED HERE BY READING BOTH RATHER THAN PICKING ONE. ***\nI recorded codex's *\"RAM does not inherently make PATH B worse\"* as a correction of my summary.  grok says\nthe opposite in its heading: *\"PATH B is worse than the brief said.\"*  **Reading both: they are not in\nconflict.**  grok's point is that `#95' does not run AT ALL on a RAM destination, so nothing pre-zeroes\nanything; codex's is that a successful RAM read still DEFINES the requested prefix through `memcpy', so only\nthe suffix beyond `bufdepth' is undefined -- which is the same defect either way.  **codex is right on the\ntechnical point and grok is right about the brief's framing.**  Recording only one of them, as I did, loses\nthat.\n\n*** THE THREE PRECEDENTS VERIFIED, AND THE THIRD IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN PRIOR RULING ON THE SAME\nQUESTION.  Checked 2026-09-07 -- grok said `grep, not argument', so they were grepped. ***\n\n    buf[3] = 0;                         dev_sgi_re.c, this function's own RGB PUT      CONFIRMED\n    memset(buf, 0, 8);                  dev_dreamcast_maple.c, twice (:285, :330)      CONFIRMED\n    *r = *g = *b = 0;                   dev_sgi_gbe.c #196                             CONFIRMED\n\n**The `#196' comment is the decisive one and it is BY THIS PROJECT'S OWN SEATS:**\n\n    default:/*  #196: (Codex/Fable) d->color_mode is guest-controlled; an\n                unimplemented WID mode must not exit() the host (get_rgb is\n                per-pixel, so don't fatal()-flood either) -- render black.  */\n            *r = *g = *b = 0;\n\n**That is the same question already answered**: a GUEST-CONTROLLED unimplemented colour mode, in a PER-PIXEL\nhelper, on a SIBLING SGI framebuffer -- and the ruling was DEFINED ZEROS, with the per-pixel property given\nexplicitly as the reason not to `fatal()'-flood.  It is the identical reasoning `sgirandompixel' used for its\nlatch, so the house doctrine is consistent across three rounds.\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BY TRANSFERRED WARRANT RATHER THAN BY PREFERENCE:** full-object zero, with a\ncomment saying plainly that hardware padding is unknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME\nmodel.  Both file-reading seats reached it independently, and `#196' is a fourth vote from a round that\nalready shipped.\n\n*This is the option-set rule working exactly as written -- `GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM; the tree's\nown precedents are part of the design space'.  I did not do that; grok did, and it produced a better answer\nthan the argument I was assembling.*\n\n*** FIFTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER -- THE PACKET-FED SEATS.  glm's contains a DEFECT NOBODY ELSE FOUND\n(filed as [[sgilinearnegidx]]) and a BETTER VERSION OF MY OWN #95 ARGUMENT. ***\n\n**glm's `#95' warrant is stronger than the one I wrote and does not depend on reading the source:**\n*\"zeroing past `len' would itself be an OOB write on any caller with a tight buffer ... So every\nMEMORY-SAFE implementation of `#95' zeroes at most `bufdepth' bytes, and PATH B's `buf[bufdepth..3]' are\nstale under all of them, not merely the described one.  PATH A is a `MEM_WRITE' whose source is `buf'; a\nwrite-side pre-zero cannot exist there without destroying the payload of every device write in the tree.\nSurvival against `#95' is OVER-DETERMINED, not lucky.\"*  My version rested on the pre-zero happening to be\ngated on `MEM_READ'; glm's shows no memory-safe implementation could behave otherwise.\n\n**And a correction to my own §3b:** I wrote that `#95' zeroes `buf[0..bufdepth-1]' unconditionally.  glm:\n*\"that is the device branch only -- the stronger case presented as fact, harmless to the claim.\"*\n\n**minimax's witness-table point, recorded with its own arithmetic slip corrected:** it observes that the\nwitness table lists only RGBA mode words and omits the CI cases, which is fair -- CI leaks at any depth above\n1 and no row drives it.  Its example value is wrong, though: it calls `0x1210' *LIN_A | depth 2 | CI*, and\n`0x1210 & 0xf0' is `0x10' = RGB, not CI (`0x00').  **The observation stands and the constant does not.**\n\n*** SHARPENED 2026-09-07 BY THE glm SEAT on the `sgirergbroundtrip` pass-1 panel (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055), and it is\nwider than this row said. ***\n\n    \"per the code's own #100 comment a bufdepth of 8 makes every named PUT case transmit four\n     uninitialized stack bytes to guest memory; a bufdepth-8 GET silently drops the high half;\n     and with bufdepth 1 or 2 the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read uninitialized buf[2..3].\"\n\n**THREE DISTINCT EXPOSURES, not one**, and the row had only the first:\n  (a) bufdepth 8 PUT -- `buf[4..7]` are never written by ANY arm, so four uninitialised stack bytes\n      go to guest memory through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)`;\n  (b) bufdepth 8 GET -- the high half is read into buf and then silently discarded, since every GET\n      arm reads at most four bytes.  Not a leak; a correctness gap;\n  (c) **bufdepth 1 or 2 GET -- the RGB, RGBA and ABGR arms read `buf[2..3]` that `memory_rw` never\n      filled.**  This is the one the row did not have, and it is the mirror of (a): a LEAK INTO THE\n      GUEST'S OWN PIXEL VALUE rather than into guest memory.\n\n**THE GUARD/BODY SPLIT IS WHY ALL THREE EXIST:** the colour type is bits 4-7 of the mode word and\n`bufdepth` is bits 8-9 of the SAME word, read independently.  Nothing couples them, so a guest\nselects a four-byte colour type with a one-byte transfer length whenever it likes.\n\n*Recorded from `#505`'s pre-table GUARD-FIELD-vs-BODY-FIELD check, which found the same disagreeing\nstate independently and then DECLINED to row it -- the exposure is identical before and after that\nround's edit (the shipped GET read three bytes, the fixed one reads three bytes), so a row there\nwould attribute a pre-existing defect to a round that neither created nor cured it.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 -- BY grok, BECAUSE CODEX REFUSED THE BRIEF.\n`regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/grok_pass1_20260907.txt`, 18 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the right fix for\nTHIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.  It is not the packing fix, it is not PUT-only, and\nno single-access guest value of 0 can pin it.  S1 and S5 hold.  S2's decode holds.  S3's GET\noverstates RGB.  S4's quotes exist and its site-count does not.\"*\n\n**Q1/Q2 ANSWERED, and Q2's cost objection dissolved by a fact I did not have:** *\"It is declared\nAFTER the coordinate / `tlb_mode` / `tileptr`-valid early returns, so a zero-fill at that site is not\npaid on declined pixels.\"*  So the per-pixel cost applies only to pixels that were going to be\ntransferred anyway -- which removes the reason to prefer a narrower site.\n\n**Q3 ANSWERED, and it is the answer I asked for rather than the one I wanted:** *\"no single-access\nguest value of `0` can pin it.\"*  A value oracle cannot distinguish \"filled with zeros\" from\n\"happened to be zeros\", which is what the brief suspected and what the round must now design around.\n\n*** MY S3 WAS WRONG IN ITS EMPHASIS, AND grok's ENUMERATION IS BETTER THAN MY PROSE. ***  I led with\ndepth 8; the severe case is CI:\n\n    PUT bytes transferred with no store:\n                              depth 1   depth 2   depth 4     depth 8\n      CI                      none      buf[1]    buf[1..3]   buf[1..7]\n      RGB/RGBA/ABGR/default   none      none      none        buf[4..7]\n\n**CI at depth 4 already transfers three unstored bytes**, which needs no unusual depth at all -- and\nthe MTE path forces CI (see [[sgimtedepth4dead]]).  *\"emphasis on depth 8 is the weak vehicle.\"*\n\n**S2 REFINED, and the refinement matters for how this is described:** on the MTE path the colour bits\nare NOT a second guest field.  `src_mode`/`dst_mode` are built as `(tlb << 10) + (depth_field << 8)`\nwith no type bits, so `color_mode` is pinned at 0 (CI) unless the dead branch fires.  *\"That is a\nconstant colliding with a guest depth, not two independent guest fields.\"*  The two-independent-fields\nstory is true of the DE path and false of the MTE path.\n\n*** TWO NEW FINDINGS UNDER THE FOUND-SOMETHING-ELSE LICENCE: ***\n  1. **`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` (bits 2-3) is never read by `dev_sgi_re.c` at all.**  A third mode field\n     the device ignores entirely.\n  2. **A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, verified by reading:** `dev_sgi_re.c:732` says\n     `// bufdepth = 1, 2, or 4.` immediately above\n     `int src_bufdepth = 1 << ((src_mode >> 8) & 3);` -- which yields **1, 2, 4 or 8**.  The `#100`\n     round grew `buf[8]` precisely because the decode reaches 8, so this comment was already false\n     when that round shipped.  **L4, cheap, and it belongs to whichever round next touches this\n     file.**\n\n**S4's SITE COUNT IS WRONG AND ITS QUOTES ARE NOT.**  I wrote that `memory_rw.c` zero-fills at three\nsites; grok says the count does not hold and flagged in its own trace that *\"GET versus PUT may not\nshare a `#95` path\"*.  **That distinction is load-bearing and I had glossed it:** `#95` zero-fills a\nREAD buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access.  It therefore does nothing for a PUT (a write), and\non a successful GET it fills exactly `bufdepth` bytes -- never `buf[2..3]` beyond them.  **So the\ntransferred warrant is weaker than the brief claimed: `#95` establishes the fork's ETHOS for this\nclass, not a mechanism that already covers these calls.**\n\n*Recorded as the round's own premise-audit result: of five settled premises, two survived intact, one\nwas refined, one had its emphasis corrected, and one had its supporting count refuted.*\n\n*** A SECOND, INDEPENDENT AUDIT AGREES ON THE FIX AND GOES FURTHER ON THE ORACLE.  codex, via the\nREWORDED brief (`codex_reworded_20260907.txt`) after the original was refused. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Zero-initialize all eight bytes at the existing declaration.  That completes the local\ninitialization repair in both directions, but does not establish correct pixel-format or MTE\nbehavior.  **No guest-visible value or write-count oracle can reliably prove initialization\noccurred.**\"*\n\n**TWO SEATS, TWO TRANSPORTS, SAME FIX.**  grok: *\"Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the\nright fix for THIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.\"*  codex: *\"Zero-initialize all eight\nbytes at the existing declaration.\"*  Both add the same caveat -- it repairs initialisation and does\nNOT repair the packing/format defect, which is [[sgimtedepth4dead]]'s territory.  **The design is\nsettled.**\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS HARDER THAN THE NEIGHBOURING ROUND ASSUMED, AND THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING FINDING.\n***  grok said no single-access guest VALUE can pin it.  codex rules out the WRITE-COUNT oracle as\nwell -- and a write-count oracle is exactly what a seat proposed for [[sgirandomcolour]]\n(*\"observe ZERO MEM_WRITE calls from an unsupported PUT\"*).  **That proposal survives for\n`sgirandomcolour`, where the question is whether a write HAPPENS; it does not transfer to this row,\nwhere the question is what the written bytes CONTAIN.**  Two rounds, one idiom, and it is valid in\nonly one of them.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** if no black-box guest-visible oracle can prove\ninitialisation, this round's detector cannot be a rung-3 probe of the ordinary kind.  The candidates\nare an offline differential over the arms, or a `harness:` witness under the ladder's fifth term.\n**That must be decided BEFORE the fix, not after** -- the round is otherwise a one-line edit with no\nway to defend it.\n\n**AND THE CEILING FOR THIS ROW IS NOW STATED CLEANLY** (same answer, Q1): *\"initializing the buffer\nchanges neither the transfer length nor the call count.  Uninitialized bytes can happen to match the\nexpected initialized values, so even matching values PLUS matching counts cannot establish\ninitialization.  **Unexpected values can expose a failure; expected values cannot prove\ndefinedness.**\"*\n\nThat is the sharpest form of the limit and it settles the round's shape: **no guest-visible oracle\ncan prove this fix worked.**  The detector must be an offline differential or a `harness:` witness\nunder the ladder's fifth term -- decided BEFORE the one-line edit, not after.\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_130159`).  SIX SEATS, ZERO DOWN, glm did\nNOT burn for the second panel running. ***\n\n**THE SPLIT, which is the thing for the adjudication:**\n  * **grok: TAKE THE ROUND** -- *\"zero-fill `buf` at the existing declaration; do not make the arms\n    write `bufdepth` bytes; do not treat a guest 0 as proof of initialisation.\"*\n  * **deepseek: NOT RATIFY as a correctness fix** -- *\"zero-fill only converts uninitialized reads\n    into defined-but-wrong bytes for CI depth>1; the independent assignment/transfer defect\n    remains.\"*\n  * **minimax: the symptom is handled but THE LICENCE IS UNSUPPORTED** -- *\"no measured delta is\n    offered.\"*\n\n  *So the question is not whether to zero-fill.  It is whether zero-fill is a FIX or a SYMPTOM PATCH\n  that leaves the real decoupling defect in place -- and whether the addition licence permits it with\n  no measured delta.*\n\n*** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND A DEFECT IN MY PACKET, AND IT IS A CROSS-ROUND INTERACTION I HAD\nNOT SEEN. ***  S3's REVERSE-DIRECTION sentence -- *\"at depth 1 or 2 the read fills only that many\nbytes and the multi-byte arms then read `buf[2..3]` regardless\"* -- is **false of RGB as the file now\nsits**.  grok: *\"its GET sentence is false of RGB.\"*  glm: *\"asserted in prose, not in the enumerated\ntable, and under the packet's own model it is wrong.\"*  minimax: *\"S3's table is asymmetric -- write\ndirection enumerated, read direction mentioned in one sentence only.\"*\n\n  **WHY: `#505` changed RGB GET from `buf[1..3]` to `buf[0..2]`.**  So post-`#505` an RGB read at\n  depth 2 touches `buf[2]` and NOT `buf[3]`.  The sentence was true of the pre-`#505` file and I\n  carried it forward into a packet written after the fix.  **My own two rounds interact and the\n  enumerated table hid it, because I enumerated the WRITE direction and prosed the READ direction.**\n  *A table beside a sentence invites exactly this: the table gets checked and the sentence does not.*\n\n**THREE SEATS ALSO AGREE S4's WARRANT IS DEAD:** *\"S4 is ethos, not precedent\"* (deepseek, minimax);\n*\"its site-count and the `#95` transfer do not [hold]\"* (grok).  I had already recorded `#95` as\nweaker than claimed; the panel makes it unanimous.  **The addition licence therefore wants a MEASURED\nDELTA for this one-line change, and none exists.**  That is now the round's blocking question rather\nthan the oracle.\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AGAINST DEPTH-AWARE ARMS**, by two seats and for the same reason: *\"depth-aware arms\nwould trespass on the neighbouring rows that own pixel semantics and would require hardware truth\nthis packet does not contain\"* (kimi).  So the boundary with [[sgimtedepth4dead]] and\n[[sgirandomcolour]] holds.\n\n*** AND THE ORACLE QUESTION HAS ITS FIRST CONSTRUCTIVE ANSWER, from the kimi substitute: ***\n*\"The only honest definedness detector is an instrumented run (MSan/Valgrind class); any harness\nwitness is one-sided **UNLESS THE STACK BENEATH THE FRAME IS PRE-SEEDED**.\"*\n\n  **That is actionable and nobody had proposed it.**  Every earlier answer said no oracle can prove\n  definedness; this says a harness witness becomes TWO-SIDED if the frame's stack is seeded with a\n  known non-zero pattern first -- then `0` means initialised and the pattern means not.  This fork\n  already has an ASAN/UBSan tree (`build-asan/`), so the instrumented route is also available.\n  **Both need checking before the round; neither was on the table an hour ago.**\n\n*** THE CODEX SEAT REFUTED S4 AND THE OTHER THREE SEATS RATIFIED MY ERROR.  SETTLED BY OPENING\nTHE FILE, NOT BY COUNTING SEATS. ***\n\nMy S4 said `#95` *\"fills a READ buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access\"*.  deepseek, minimax and\ngrok all agreed and graded the warrant *\"ethos, not precedent\"*.  **codex went and read it:**\n\n    memory_rw.c :492   if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);\n                :494   paddr -= mem->devices[i].baseaddr;      <- THE DEVICE DISPATCH\n\n**`:492` is not in a failure branch at all.**  It prefills on EVERY device read, successful or not,\nbefore dispatch and before length clamping -- its own comment says *\"never hand back uninitialised\nhost memory to the guest.  Root-cause fix for the uninit-read class\"*.  The failure-path fills are\n`:215` and `:267`, the two that `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED`.  **Verified here by reading all six\nsites.**  codex's enumeration of six (215, 267, 492, 671, 683, 732) is correct and my 'three sites'\nwas wrong as well.\n\n  > **SO THE WARRANT IS A PRECEDENT, NOT AN ETHOS: `#95` is exactly 'initialise storage before a\n  > producer may assign only part of it'.**  That is this fix, one layer down.  The addition licence\n  > is satisfied by TRANSFERRED WARRANT and the round does NOT owe a measured delta -- which\n  > REVERSES the blocking question three seats had just created.\n\n**THIS IS THE WRONG-FACT-COMES-BACK-CONFIRMED CLASS AT FULL STRENGTH.**  I stated a weakened S4 as a\nSETTLED PREMISE; three of six seats confirmed it; one seat checked it.  The carrier's rule --\n*unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing; score agreement only where a seat re-derived\nit* -- scores this exactly right, and I had written the premise-audit opener into the brief and\nstill stated S4 as settled rather than as a question.\n\n*The sharper lesson: **a premise I had ALREADY CORRECTED ONCE, in the weakening direction, is the\nmost dangerous kind.**  It arrives wearing the clothes of a correction, so it reads as the careful\nversion and nobody re-checks it -- including me, who wrote it.*\n\n**codex's other corrections, each verified before recording:**\n  * **S1 is wrong.**  Not 'once per pixel from three callers': THREE execution paths, TWO caller\n    functions, SEVEN direct call sites (LINE GET :817 PUT :863; RECTANGLE GET :878, GET :883,\n    PUT :929; tiled MTE GET :1382 PUT :1387).  RECTANGLE does TWO GETs and a PUT per iteration, so\n    'once per pixel' understates a fill's cost threefold on that path.\n  * **S3's GET row corrected, agreeing with grok, glm and minimax** -- per-arm and per-width: CI\n    consumes NO unassigned byte at any width; RGB consumes `buf[1..2]` after a 1-byte read and\n    `buf[2]` after 2; RGBA/ABGR/default consume `buf[1..3]` and `buf[2..3]`.  **`#505` is why RGB\n    differs** -- it now reads `buf[0..2]` and never `buf[3]`.\n  * *** `-DNDEBUG` IS SET, so an `assert()`-based detector is a NO-OP in a normal build. ***\n    Verified: `configure:313` sets `CFLAGS=\"-DNDEBUG $CFLAGS\"`, and `src/devices/Makefile:9` carries\n    it.  An initialisedness harness built on `assert` would be VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the\n    check-that-cannot-fail shape, arriving inside the detector for this very round.\n  * **Q4, a coverage gap in the SHIPPED `#505` detector:** `rgb depth64` and `rgb tiled64` widen the\n    *source read*; their destination stays a four-byte RGBA write, so **no row establishes\n    initialisation of an eight-byte PUT's tail.**  Filed as [[sgireput8tail]] rather than fixed.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- ENUMERATED, not asserted, in BOTH directions. ***\n\n**PUT -- uninitialised bytes actually transferred to guest memory:**\n\n    CI          depth 2 -> 1 byte, depth 4 -> 3 bytes, depth 8 -> 7 bytes\n    every other encoding (incl. RGB/RGBA/ABGR/YCRCB and the 11 undocumented)  depth 8 -> 4 bytes\n\n    18 of 64 colour-type x byte-depth combinations, across all sixteen encodings the type mask\n    allows.  (6 of 16 counting only the four handled types; 7 of 20 including YCRCB.)\n\n**GET -- uninitialised bytes consumed by the recombination: 30 of 64.**\n\n## TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG\n\n* **`random()` INITIALISES its four bytes.**  So the `sgirandomcolour` arm does NOT leak\n  uninitialised data -- it writes objectionable data.  Different defect, and the distinction is\n  what keeps the two rows separable.\n* **RGB's GET reads `buf[0..2]` only, never `buf[3]`** -- my brief said the arms recombine\n  `buf[0..3]`, which is true of the others.\n* and my \"stale from a previous call\" framing was loose: *\"there is no persistent previous-call\n  buffer in the language semantics.  Automatic storage becomes indeterminate again on each\n  execution of the declaration.\"*\n\n## *** A REACHABILITY FINDING I DID NOT ASK FOR: THE MTE PATH HITS THIS. ***\n\nThe tiled MTE constructs **CI/1, CI/2, RGBA/4 and CI/8**.  Its GETs therefore avoid the short-read\nrecombination -- **but its CI/2 and CI/8 PUTs are affected.**  *So this is not a hypothetical\ncombination space: an in-tree path already builds two of the leaking cells.*\n\n## THE FIX, and it costs nothing on correct paths\n\n    uint8_t buf[8] = {0};\n\n*\"It preserves the output of otherwise defined paths whose consumed bytes were already initialised.\nPUT overwrites the same bytes before transferring them; GET overwrites the requested bytes before\nrecombination.  Transfer addresses and lengths remain unchanged.\"*  **That is the Q4 property --\nno currently-correct path changes.**\n\n## SEVERITY, BOUNDED RATHER THAN INFLATED -- which is what I asked for\n\n**Both a guest-visible nondeterminism defect AND a bounded information-exposure one**, but *\"source\ninspection alone does not demonstrate secrets, another process's memory, or a reliable arbitrary\nhost-memory read.\"*  It is the emulator's own stack.\n\n## SCOPE: two rounds -- AGREED, but NOT on the panel's reason\n\nThe `sgirandomcolour` panel ruled two rounds 5/5 because *\"one oracle cannot distinguish a\nCI/depth-8 regression from a YCRCB regression.\"*  codex agrees with the conclusion and **disputes\nthe argument**: *\"One test suite can check multiple independent properties; one CI/depth-8\nobservation cannot establish YCRCB correctness.\"*\n\n**And it sharpened the overlap the panel had called a half-fix.**  Zero-initialisation fixes the\nfour unwritten TAIL bytes of the default PUT at depth 8, and the default GET's short-read\nrecombination -- **it leaves all four `random()` calls and their PRNG-state effects intact.**  So it\nis an overlap in *uninitialised-buffer behaviour*, not half of the random assignments.\n**RECORDED HERE EXPLICITLY, as it asked**: if `sgirandomcolour` claims those tail/GET cases, its\nscope overlaps this row and the two rounds must say so.\n\n## *** THE ORACLE IS THE HONEST ANSWER I ASKED FOR, AND IT HAS A COST ***\n\n*\"A single ordinary run cannot reliably distinguish fixed from unfixed.  Uninitialised storage can\nhappen to contain zeros or a stable pattern ... Repeated identical output also does not prove\ninitialisation.\"*\n\n**The instrument is MemorySanitizer**, which can tell an uninitialised zero from a deliberately\ninitialised one.  **THAT IS A NEW BUILD TREE:** `build-asan/` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined`, and **MSan is incompatible with ASan** -- so this round owes a third\nsanitizer configuration, not a re-use.  *That cost is real and is recorded now rather than\ndiscovered mid-round.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31674-   "entries": [
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31675-    {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31676-     "date": "2026-09-07",
--
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32089-   "entries": []
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32090-  },
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32091-  {
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:32092:   "id": "lcaisalane",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32093-   "sev": "med",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32094-   "state": "open",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32095-   "opened": "2026-09-07",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32096-   "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): THREE defects in eleven lines -- an off-by-one guard that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros, a discarded downstream failure result, and an uninitialised byte consumed on that failure",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32097-   "witness": null,
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:32098:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  The lane-semantics half was raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) with an\nenumerated table; the third defect I found by reading the function while checking that table.\n\n    ofs = relative_addr & 3;                       <- guest address bits 5-6\n    if (ofs > len) { fatal(...); exit(1); }        <- `>' where `>=' was meant\n    ...\n    uint8_t byte;                                  <- never initialised\n    cpu->memory_rw(..., &byte, 1, MEM_READ, ...);  <- result DISCARDED\n    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)\n            data[i] = i == ofs ? byte : 0x00;\n\n**1. OFF-BY-ONE.**  The guard is `>' rather than `>=', so ofs == len passes.  codex's table, re-derived:\n\n    len=1  ofs {0,1} accepted      len=4  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n    len=2  ofs {0,1,2} accepted    len=8  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n\nFor (len,ofs) = (1,1) and (2,2) the loop never reaches i == ofs, so **the access performs a real downstream\nread and returns ALL ZEROS**.  The guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.\n\n**2. THE READ PATH DISCARDS THE FAILURE RESULT** that the WRITE path returns -- codex's observation, and the\nasymmetry is visible with the two branches side by side.\n\n**3. `byte' IS UNINITIALISED and is consumed if that discarded read failed.**  This is the FOURTH instance\nof the uninitialised-consumption family recorded on 2026-09-07, alongside [[sgirebufuninit]],\n[[sgigbeconvover]] and #95's incomplete class closure.  **#95 zeroes device READ buffers, so a\ndevice-served read is covered; a read that FAILS before reaching a handler is not** -- the same gap\nsgirebufuninit turns on.\n\nMED rather than HIGH: the wrong-lane read is a correctness defect with no host death and nothing disclosed\nbut a stale stack byte.  The exit(1) in the same function is [[lcabadval]]'s range site, graded there.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of the (1,1)/(2,2) pairs is established by reading; nothing shows a guest driving\nthem.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08.  I DELIBERATELY DID NOT TELL IT WHAT THE THREE DEFECTS WERE, and\nwarned it not to manufacture three.  It found SIX items and said the filed count is wrong. ***\n\n*\"Four behavioral issues, one conditional initialization gap, and one minor format mismatch.  **The\nfiled 'three' overstates the initialization claim.**\"*\n\n## *** THE TRAP: THE OBVIOUS ONE-CHARACTER FIX MAKES IT WORSE ***\n\n    dev_lca.c:193    if (ofs > len) {          <- equality PASSES\n\nOn equality the write selects byte zero via `ofs % len` and the read never places the downstream\nbyte anywhere -- **but the downstream read HAS OCCURRED and may have side effects.**  The guard\nshould be `ofs >= len`.\n\n**BUT the rejected-lane arm is `exit(1)` at :196, so changing only `>` to `>=` EXPANDS the\ntermination.**  *That is the same shape as `alphavmkill`, where deleting a kill would have published\na bogus 4 TB mapping: the boundary repair and the disposition repair are one design or neither.*\n\n**AND IT BOUNDED THE CLAIM RATHER THAN INFLATING IT:** *\"This is NOT a demonstrated buffer\noverrun\"* -- positive-length writes use modulo and the read loop stays within `len`; the modulo is\npart of the boundary behaviour, not an independent OOB.\n\n## THE IDIOM CENSUS -- five files, grepped rather than argued\n\n* **the same function's WRITE arm already returns the downstream result** -- the strongest precedent\n  for repairing its read arm;\n* both siblings (`lca_pci_conf`, `lca_ioc`) initialise `odata = 0`, and the config handler has a\n  non-terminating `dev = 0` fallback;\n* `eagle` and `footbridge_isa` initialise read results, and footbridge warns on unsupported writes\n  without exiting -- *but footbridge is not an equivalent sparse-lane mapper*;\n* `DEV_RAM_MIRROR` is the closest FORWARDING precedent: passes `PHYSICAL`, returns the nested result;\n* `wdc` -- ISA-connected -- already declines and latches for unsupported widths.\n* **no second implementation of LCA's sparse-lane packing exists in the fork.**\n\n## SIX ITEMS, each with disposition, bounded severity, and its observable\n\n    1 lane boundary        member 3   MED source-only   downstream access occurrence + payload\n                                                        (readback zero ALONE cannot distinguish)\n    2 process exit         member 3   MED pending witness -- and ALREADY RECORDED under lcabadval\n    3 lost read failure    member 7   LOW/documented    handler return status, writeback suppression\n    4 initialisation gap   member 7   LOW/documented    definedness analysis; normal readback is NOT\n                                                        an established channel\n    5 physical forwarding  member 7   MED conditional   which backing is accessed; bootstrap masks it\n    6 diagnostic format    member 7   LOW portability   no runtime text difference established\n\n**Member 5 FAULT is unavailable**, confirmed for the third time this session from a different file:\n`memory_rw.c`'s device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K arms and **no Alpha arm**, and\n`ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` is defined and unused.\n\n## *** THE DUPLICATE I ASKED IT TO LOOK FOR -- AND THERE IS ONE ***\n\n*\"The original `lcabadval` scope explicitly EXCLUDES range-guard analysis, while its later note\nrecords this exact termination site.  **Extract or reassign that site explicitly rather than counting\nit twice.**\"*  `lcaconfbus1kill` and `lcairqstubkill` remain separate; their severity and witnesses\ndo not transfer here.  **This is an open obligation on both rows and is not discharged by recording\nit here.**\n\n## SCOPE: SEVERAL ROUNDS\n\nBoundary + termination are **one** design (same guard site).  Read completion is another; physical\nforwarding another.  *\"Sharing a function -- or a prospective test executable -- does not establish\none semantic class and one oracle.\"*\n\n## NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE IS CHEAP\n\n*\"I found no in-tree regression exercising the sparse `lca_isa` handler.\"*  The config probes cover\nother handlers, and `lca_irqstub_probe.py` **accesses the downstream ISA mapping directly, bypassing\nthis shim.**  So every item here is a documented source candidate until someone drives the shim.",
