tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32117-   "opened": "2026-09-07",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32118-   "title": "dev_lca.c: 0x800 << dev with dev reaching 20 evaluates 2^31 in a signed 32-bit int -- undefined behaviour, in the loop the fork's own comment treats as the safe path",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32119-   "witness": "probe:regress/lca_confbus_probe.py against build-asan/gxemul -- rung 3, TWO-DIRECTION: HEAD emits \"left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be represented in type int\" at dev_lca.c:125:21 (ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw, 1 diagnostic); FIXED emits NONE (ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw, 0).  Same script, opposite outcome.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:32120:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n**THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED WITNESS** and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) is gradeable.\nThe repair is COMPLETE for this site: `0x800U` makes the operand unsigned, so `2048U << 20` is\n`0x80000000U`, well inside `unsigned int`, and the `tag &` comparison is unaffected because `tag` is\nalready a wider unsigned quantity.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS LOW and that is deliberate**: the overflow is UB by the language, the emitted code\non every compiler this project targets is what was intended, and no wrong guest-visible value has\nbeen measured.  What changed is that it is now WITNESSED rather than argued.\n\n*** THE FIX IS DESIGNED AND EXECUTABLE.  Written out here so the next session can run it rather than\nre-derive it -- the round is small and every input is now in hand. ***\n\n**THE EDIT, one token:**\n\n    -\tif (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n    +\tif (tag & (0x800U << dev))\t/*  #NNN  */\n\n`0x800U` makes the operand `unsigned int`, so `2048U << 20` is `0x80000000U` -- well inside\n`unsigned int` -- and no other behaviour moves: `tag` is already a wider unsigned quantity, so the\n`&` and the truth value are unchanged at every one of the 21 iterations.  **The emitted code on\nevery compiler this project targets is what was already intended**, which is why the severity is LOW\neven though the language calls it undefined.\n\n*** THE TEST-FIRST ORDER IS ALREADY SATISFIED, WHICH IS UNUSUAL AND WORTH NOTING. ***  The witness\nexists and is RED on the committed build: running `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` against\n`build-asan/gxemul` emits\n\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n\nSo the reproduce-before-you-edit step is DONE and preserved\n(`regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`).  The post-fix half is: the same command,\nsame binary path, and **the diagnostic must be ABSENT**.  Same script, opposite outcome -- which is\nthe ladder's clause (i) exactly.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND OWES, and the one real decision in it:**\n\nThe two-direction control above is a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL, and this project has that filed as its own\nround ([[ubsanshiftlane]]) because it is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE -- a second binary and a STDERR\noracle rather than a value oracle.  **So the honest choice is:**\n\n* **(a) ship the fix with the UBSAN differential as its detector**, which means building at least a\n  minimal version of the lane in this round -- and a lane added inside a fix round is a lane nobody\n  reviewed, which is the exact objection `ubsanshiftlane` was filed to avoid; or\n* **(b) ship the fix with a SOURCE PIN** -- a check asserting the expression is `0x800U` -- and\n  leave the differential to its own round.  `#510`'s own product comments already name a source pin\n  as the legitimate alternative to UBSAN for precisely this class (\"No row can kill a `1U' -> `1'\n  regression; UBSAN or a source pin can\").\n\n**(b) is in scope and (a) is not**, on this project's own stopping rule: the fix is a one-token\ncorrectness repair, and expanding it into a new measurement lane is the scope creep the rule exists\nto refuse.  *Recorded as a reasoned choice rather than a preference, so a reviewer can disagree with\nthe reason.*\n\n**COST: a build.**  The edit must propagate to all four trees and `build-asan` must be REBUILT for\nthe post-fix measurement, which is the only expensive part and is why this was not executed the\nmoment it was designed -- a rebuild must not overlap a running seat.\n\n**AND THE POST-FIX RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX.**  If the diagnostic does NOT\ndisappear, the repair is wrong or the site is not the one being reported, and that is a finding\nabout the measurement rather than a formality.  Predict-then-measure: it should vanish, and nothing\nelse in the probe's four rows should move (3/4 before, 3/4 after -- the `bus kill` row is a\ndifferent defect and stays RED).\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 2026-09-07, as `#511`. ***\n\n    HEAD    1 diagnostic     ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw\n    FIXED   0 diagnostics    ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw\n    control  build/ scores 3/4 before AND after, same three rows green, `bus kill' still RED\n             (a different defect) -- NOTHING ELSE MOVED\n\n**The pre-registered prediction held exactly**: the diagnostic vanished and no other row moved.  All\nfour trees cleared two-part after the propagate (`est/`: source tree, part 2 N/A).\n\n**The detector is the differential itself and it is NOT GATE-WIRED**, which the CHANGELOG block says\nin those words.  A source pin was considered and rejected: it would test the TEXT while this round\nhas the BEHAVIOUR.  Gate-wiring is [[ubsanshiftlane]], deliberately separate.\n\n*** THE SIBLING QUESTION IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE PANEL ANSWERS IT, AND THE RESULT IS A CLEAN\nABSENCE -- RECORDED WITH ITS WARRANT SO IT IS FALSIFIABLE. ***\n\n`#511`'s own pass-2 brief asks (Q5) whether a sibling of this shape was missed.  Censused:\n\n    signed-constant  <<  VARIABLE, tree-wide                       317 sites\n    of those, literal > 1 AND headroom <= 24                          2 sites\n\n**317 IS A CORRELATE, NOT THE CLASS.**  `1 << x` with 31 places of headroom is the ordinary bitmask\nidiom in C and is a defect only if `x` can reach 31.  The `#511` SHAPE is distinctive: a literal\nLARGER than 1 (so little headroom) shifted by a variable that a loop can drive to the overflow point.\nFiltering on that leaves TWO:\n\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:772   tmp &= 0x0fff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 20\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:774   tmp &= 0xffff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 16\n\n**BOTH ARE SAFE, and the warrant is a compile-time constant:**\n\n    src/include/cop0.h:85    #define PAGEMASK_SHIFT   13\n    0x0fff << 13 = 0x01ffe000       0xffff << 13 = 0x1fffe000       INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff\n\n`PAGEMASK_SHIFT` is a MACRO, not a runtime variable, so the compiler folds the whole expression and\nthere is no shift to overflow.  **My regex matched an identifier and could not tell a macro from a\nvariable** -- which is why the two hits needed reading rather than counting.\n\n> **[THIS ABSENCE RECORD IS FALSE.  See the correction at the end of this note -- the grok pass-2\n> seat found TWO siblings my filter had hidden.]**  ~~no other site in the tree has a signed literal > 1 shifted by a value that can reach 2^31.~~  THE WARRANT IS\n> `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` AND ITS BEING A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.**  If either changes -- the value\n> raised past 15, or the macro replaced by a variable -- the two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites become\n> live and this record expires.\n\n*A near-miss worth keeping: my first attempt grepped `define PAGEMASK_SHIFT` in `src/` and returned\nNOTHING, and I had already computed the arithmetic assuming 13.  Zero hits is not evidence, and the\nnumber I was about to rely on was a guess.  It IS 13 -- found in `src/include/cop0.h` on a broader\nsearch -- but the conclusion was reached before the fact was.*\n\n*** THE ABSENCE RECORD ABOVE IS FALSE, AND MY OWN FILTER IS WHAT MADE IT SO.  The grok pass-2 seat\nfound TWO SIBLINGS, both verified here. ***\n\ngrok's verdict on `#511` closes with: *\"Two other PCI IDSEL scanners still evaluate `1 << 31`.\"*\nVerified by reading:\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:70   for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n                 :71       if (idata & (1 << dev))            <- dev REACHES 31\n    dev_v3.c:164        for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)\n            :165           if (relative_addr & (1 << dev))    <- dev REACHES 31\n\n    1 << 31 = 0x80000000 = 2147483648    INT_MAX = 2147483647    OVERFLOWS\n\n**Both are the IDENTICAL SHAPE to `#511`** -- a signed literal shifted by a loop variable the loop\nbound drives to the overflow point -- and both loops explicitly run to `dev < 32`, so 31 is reached\nwhenever no earlier bit matches.\n\n*** THE FILTER THAT HID THEM WAS MINE, AND IT WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS. ***  My census excluded\n`if v <= 1: continue`, reasoning that ``1 << x`` is the ordinary bitmask idiom in C and only a\ndefect if `x` reaches 31.  **That reasoning is right and the exclusion is wrong**: `1` has the MOST\nheadroom (31 places), which makes it the ONLY literal that overflows at the maximum shift a\n`< 32` loop can produce.  I filtered on \"little headroom = suspicious\" and thereby removed the\nsingle literal whose headroom exactly matches the commonest loop bound in the tree.\n\n> **AN ABSENCE RECORD IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE FILTER THAT PRODUCED IT, AND MY WARRANT NAMED THE\n> WRONG THING.**  I recorded the warrant as `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` -- a fact about the two sites the\n> filter DID surface -- when the load-bearing assumption was the FILTER ITSELF.  A warrant should\n> name what would falsify the record, and what falsified it was the exclusion, not the constant.\n\n**The `PAGEMASK_SHIFT` half stands**: those two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites really are safe, for the\nreason given.  What is retracted is the universal claim that nothing else in the tree has the shape.\n\n*Filed to [[shiftubcensus]], which is the row for this class and was already marked HONESTLY\nPARTIAL.  Recorded here too because THIS row is where the false absence was written.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-32121-   "entries": [],
