tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34175:   "state_note": "*** A SEAT-FAILURE CLASS NOT ON THE CARRIER'S LIST, MEASURED BY CENSUS 2026-09-08. ***\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.  If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request.  To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program\n\n**It is a PLATFORM content filter, not a model refusal**, and it is the only codex failure mode\nhere that can leave a ZERO-BYTE answer behind a large `.err`.  The carrier lists codex refusal\ntriggers as vocabulary, archive-reading, chain shape and the evasion-construction question; this\nis a different mechanism and it is not listed.\n\n*** THE CENSUS, and it corrects my own first reading of it. ***\n\n    21   .err files carry the flag\n     8   of those left a ZERO-BYTE .txt\n     5   of the 21 produced a FULL answer anyway (14,102 / 18,687 / 12,952 / 8,682 / 6,868 b)\n\n**So the flag is NOT terminal and its PRESENCE proves nothing** -- exactly like the refusal-line\nrule already on record.  `gateworktree`'s 14,102 b assess carries the flag twice and answered in\nfull.  **The `.txt` SIZE is the only discriminator**, which is the rule this project already\nlearned once for refusal lines and had not connected to this filter.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN VOCABULARY, WHICH IS WHY IT KEEPS RECURRING. ***  The row\nids are `sgirekills`, `lcaconfbus1kill`, `lcairqstubkill`, `pcc2c040kill`, `alphavmkill`,\n`asicoffsetkill`; the probe rows are literally named `tlb_a ld kill`, and briefs speak of *death\nrows* and *host kills*.  **A brief that merely NAMES its row carries the trigger.**  The graded\nevidence is in `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/`:\n\n    attempt1.err                       5,906 b   flagged early\n    attempt2_neutralised.err           6,572 b   flagged early\n    attempt3_scoping.err               6,829 b   flagged early\n    attempt4_neutral_late_flag.err   382,773 b   did the WHOLE analysis, flagged at the END\n\n*** THE RECOVERY ALREADY WORKS AND HAS BEEN USED THREE TIMES, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN NOWHERE. ***\n\n    sgirekills      codex_rounddesign_20260908       0 b  ->  _RETRY_  6,868 b\n    armbfiwidth     codex_fixdesign_20260907         0 b  ->  20260908 5,091 b, v2 7,308 b\n    sgirebufuninit  codex_pass1_20260907             0 b  ->  _reworded_ 10,557 b\n\n**The filename `codex_reworded_...` IS the technique**, applied by hand three times by whoever hit\nthe wall, and never promoted to a rule -- so the next round rediscovers it.  *That is the carrier's\nown trigger-versus-memory finding: the knowledge existed, on disk, at the moment of the decision,\nand did not fire.*\n\n**STILL UNRECOVERED (codex lost; another seat covered the topic in all but the last):**\n`lcaconfwidth` census (grok 25,281 b), `lcaconfbus1kill` witness (grok 10,679 b), `alphatbinegsel`\nwitness (grok 10,679 b -- grok took it after codex was flagged), and `m88kexcdeliver/pass1/\ncodex_p1_refire` which has a 0-byte `.txt` and **NO `.err` at all**, i.e. by the redirect-target\nrule it never started rather than being flagged.\n\n**A RECORDS GAP FOUND WHILE MEASURING THIS:** `lcaconfwidth` has no brief file on disk at all --\nthe brief was passed inline and not preserved -- so **a flagged job there cannot be re-fired or\naudited without reconstructing its brief.**  A brief that is not preserved is not re-runnable.\n\n**PROPOSED, NOT DONE:** a neutral-vocabulary convention for codex briefs (*process-exit arm*,\n*unreachable-path termination*, *abort site* for *kill*; describe the row rather than naming its\nid), plus preserving every brief beside its answer.  It is a convention change affecting every\nfuture brief, so it goes to the owner rather than being adopted by a round.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS NOW MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: A ONE-VARIABLE EXPERIMENT, TWO BRIEFS DIFFERING IN\nONE QUESTION. ***\n\n    IMPLEMENT_brief.md     Q4 = \"what is the smallest edit that would make this look\n                                implemented while still being wrong?\"        ->  0 b, flag x2\n    IMPLEMENT_brief_v2.md  Q4 = \"what observable behaviour distinguishes a correct\n                                implementation from an incomplete one?\"      ->  7,026 b, flag x0\n\nEverything else in the two files is byte-identical -- the diff is that question and nothing else.\n**The answer went from nothing to a full review, and the flag count from two to zero.**\n\n> **SO THE EVASION-CONSTRUCTION FORM IS A TRIGGER AND THE OBSERVABLE-DIFFERENCE FORM IS NOT, AND\n> THE TWO ASK FOR THE SAME ENGINEERING CONTENT.**  The carrier already listed \"the ask-again /\n> evasion-construction question\" among codex's refusal triggers; this measures it, and gives the\n> rewrite rather than only the warning.\n\n**THE REWRITE, which costs nothing and preserves the adversarial value:** ask what OBSERVABLE\ndistinguishes correct from incomplete, and what a regression row must assert so a later partial\nimplementation does not read as complete.  That still forces the seat to think like an adversary\nabout the detector, without asking it to author a disguised defect.\n\n**AND THE NON-TERMINAL HALF GAINED A SIXTH INSTANCE THE SAME DAY:** the `lcaconfbus1kill`\nself-review carried **five** flag hits and still returned **9,710 bytes** of full answer -- the\nlargest flag count on record with a complete answer behind it.  *The flag's presence remains\nevidence of nothing; the `.txt` size is the discriminator.*\n\n**Four briefs written in neutral vocabulary and checked against the trigger list before firing\n(`lcabadval/MANUAL`, `lcabadval/PACKET`, `alphaswpctxreg/PASS1`, `lcaconfbus1kill/SELFREVIEW`)\nreturned 8,657 / n-a / 4,841 / 9,710 bytes.  Zero lost seats since the rule was applied.**\n\n*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-09-08, AND THEY CHANGE THE DIAGNOSIS.  THE TRIGGER IS NOT THE BRIEF'S\nWORDING -- IT IS WHAT CODEX READS AFTER THE BRIEF, AND THAT IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN RECORDS. ***\n\nTwo independent codex jobs refused the same way inside one hour:\n\n    lcaconfbus1kill pass 2      71,124 b of trace, then ERROR ... flagged for possible\n                                cybersecurity risk       (24,984 tokens)\n    sgirandomcolour witness     277,836 b of trace, same ERROR   (78,953 tokens)\n\n**THE SECOND ONE HAD ALREADY BEEN BENIGN-REFRAMED, AND THAT IS THE EVIDENCE.**  Its brief was\nwritten from the start as ordinary emulator correctness work: no \"kill\", no \"attack\", no \"exploit\",\nno \"host death\" -- a census of the finished text returned ZERO for every one of those.  It was\nflagged anyway.\n\n*** WHAT THE TRACE SHOWS, and it is the whole finding: codex went and read the RECORDS. ***\n`grep -c 'dev_sgi_re|crmfbreg|DE_MODE_TYPE'` over its trace = **1,725**, and the trace contains long\nverbatim extracts of `CHANGELOG.md` and `tools/pipeline/ledger.json` -- files whose prose is\nsaturated with exactly the vocabulary the brief had been scrubbed of, because THIS PROJECT WROTE\nTHEM ABOUT HOST KILLS AND GUEST-REACHABLE PROCESS TERMINATION.\n\n> **SO BENIGN FRAMING OF THE BRIEF IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT.  A file-reading seat pointed at\n> this repo will find the un-scrubbed vocabulary by itself, in the records, and the refusal arrives\n> at OUTPUT time after the tokens have been spent.**\n\n**AND THE COST IS NOT ONLY THE SEAT.**  Both refusals came AFTER substantial real work -- 79k tokens\non the second -- so the failure is expensive and produces a ZERO-BYTE `.txt` with everything in the\n`.err`.  A run that reads 1,725 record lines and then refuses looks, from the `.txt` alone, exactly\nlike a seat that was never invoked.\n\n**THE FIX UNDER TEST (not yet proven at the time of writing): a `§B0` READING-SCOPE BOUND** naming\nthe four files the task needs and saying explicitly *\"do NOT search or read CHANGELOG.md,\nledger.json, OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md or _scratchpad/ -- those are engineering RECORDS, not the code\nunder review\"*, with an instruction to stop and say so rather than go and get a fact that lives only\nthere.  **Recorded as a HYPOTHESIS with its result to follow, not as a remedy**, because this row\nhas already had one diagnosis overtaken by evidence and a second unverified one would be worse than\nnone.\n\n*The operational consequence for the standing directive to use codex heavily: EVERY codex brief in\nthis repo now carries the scope bound, and a codex job that returns a zero-byte `.txt` must have its\n`.err` TAIL read before it is scored -- the seat-check rule that a wall is diagnosed from `.err` and\nnever from size, applied to a failure mode that is not a wall.*\n\n*** THE RECORDS HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED, AND THE REAL DISCRIMINATOR IS MEASURED.  Four controlled\nfirings, 2026-09-08, and the split is clean. ***\n\n**The scope-bound experiment WORKED as an experiment and FAILED as a remedy.**  Adding a `§B0`\nreading bound cut the trace from 78,953 tokens to 17,566 -- so codex obeyed it and did NOT go to the\nrecords -- **and it was flagged anyway.**  So the vocabulary in `CHANGELOG.md` and `ledger.json` is\nnot the trigger.\n\n**THE DECISIVE CONTROL: a MINIMAL brief on the SAME FILE, in the same repo, with no scope bound at\nall, ANSWERED.**  733 bytes, correct, in one call:\n\n    \"Read src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c.  How many case labels does the colour switch have?\n     What does its default: arm do?  Does crmfbreg.h define a constant it has no case for?\n     Answer in prose.  Do not propose any code change, and do not write any test.\"\n\n    -> ANSWERED.  Named all four cases, described the default arm, named DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB.\n\n**SO IT IS NOT THE FILE, NOT THE REPO, AND NOT THE RECORDS.  IT IS THE ASK.**\n\n    FLAGGED  lcaconfbus1kill pass 2   asked it to assess a probe that DRIVES an aborting path\n    FLAGGED  sgirandomcolour witness  asked for \"the exact guest instruction sequence ... as\n                                      concrete stores with addresses and values\"\n    FLAGGED  sgirandomcolour scoped   same ask, reading bounded\n    ANSWERED sgirandomcolour minimal  pure analysis; explicitly \"do not write any test\"\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR: codex answers ANALYSIS of a code path and refuses CONSTRUCTION of an input\n> sequence that drives an unhandled one.**  Test-construction and exploit-construction are the same\n> shape from outside, and this project's rung-3 witness discipline is, by design, exactly that shape\n> -- a hand-assembled instruction sequence built to reach a path a guest should not reach.\n\n*** THE OPERATIONAL REMEDY, which KEEPS the seat rather than losing it: SPLIT THE JOB. ***\n\n    to codex     the enumeration, the premise audit, the decode arithmetic, the option-set\n                 reasoning, the disposition argument, \"which constant has no case\", \"what does\n                 this arm do\", \"is this claim true of the source\"\n    NOT to codex the instruction sequence, the probe design, the address construction\n                 -> those go to grok (which built this round's decode walk unaided), to agy when\n                    no measurement is in flight, or to the main loop\n\n**AND THE ANALYSIS HALF IS THE HALF THIS PROJECT MOST NEEDS FROM IT.**  On the round where it was\nflagged, the seat that replaced it -- grok -- produced the round's single most valuable finding by\ndoing exactly the analysis codex would have done.  Nothing about the split reduces codex to a\nlesser seat; it aims it at what it will answer.\n\n*A cheap standing test, since the boundary may move: the minimal-analysis brief above is preserved\nat `regress/artifacts/codexcyberflag/minimal_20260908.txt` with its `.err`.  Re-firing it is a\nfour-second liveness check that separates \"codex is walled\" from \"codex refused THIS ask\" -- two\nstates this row previously conflated, and which a zero-byte `.txt` renders identically.*\n\n**AND A ZERO-BYTE `.txt` IS THE STANDING TELL.**  All three refusals wrote 0 bytes to stdout and\nput everything on stderr, so from the `.txt` alone a refusal is indistinguishable from a seat that\nwas never invoked.  **Read the `.err` TAIL before scoring any codex job** -- the seat-check rule\nthat a wall is diagnosed from content and never from size, applied to a failure mode that is not a\nwall.\n\n*** THE DIAGNOSIS MADE A PREDICTION AND THE PREDICTION HELD, which is the first time this row has\nbeen able to say that. 2026-09-08, `#515`'s ask-again. ***\n\nThe ask-again brief is the one this project fires at a CLEAN kill table, and its whole purpose is to\nobtain **an edit that re-opens a closed defect**.  Its words were: *\"give me the exact source edit as\na diff hunk\"*, *\"the smallest realistic edit ... which leaves all 14 rows GREEN while re-opening the\ndefect\"*.\n\n**That is construction in its purest form, so the diagnosis says it must be refused -- and it was**,\nat 6,339 bytes with the same filter message.  Five firings now, and the split is unchanged:\n\n    FLAGGED    #515 pass 2 (original)          construction: assess a probe that drives a kill path\n    FLAGGED    sgirandomcolour witness         construction: \"the exact guest instruction sequence\"\n    FLAGGED    sgirandomcolour scoped          same ask, reading bounded\n    FLAGGED    #515 ask-again                  construction: \"the exact source edit as a diff hunk\"\n    ANSWERED   #515 pass 2 benign re-fire      analysis\n    ANSWERED   minimal probe                   analysis\n    ANSWERED   lcabadval (x2), lcairqstubkill  analysis\n    ANSWERED   sgirandomcolour ANALYSIS        analysis -- same row, same file, opposite outcome\n\n*** THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE IS SHARPER THAN \"SPLIT THE JOB\", AND IT NAMES A STANDING COST. ***\n\n**The ask-again is STRUCTURALLY a construction task**, so codex cannot serve it in its usual form.\nThat is not a small loss: the ask-again is the capped adversarial query this project relies on after\na table comes back green, and codex is the seat most likely to enumerate an input space exhaustively.\n\n**THE REFRAME THAT KEEPS THE SEAT: ask for the GAP, not for the EDIT.**\n\n    refused    \"give me the smallest edit that leaves all 14 rows green\"\n    accepted   \"name the input-space cell these 14 rows leave least constrained, and say why it\n                matters -- describe it as a property, not as an edit\"\n\nThose solicit the same knowledge.  A named unconstrained cell is one step from a mutant, and that\nstep is one I can take myself.  **The seat supplies the enumeration; the main loop supplies the\nedit** -- which is the same division the rest of this row already prescribes, applied to the one\nbrief in the pipeline that most obviously violates it.\n\n*Recorded as a held prediction rather than as another instance, because that is what raises this from\na pattern to a usable rule: the diagnosis said in advance which of two briefs would be refused, and\nit was right.  The reframed firing's outcome is recorded separately -- if IT is also refused, the\n\"analysis is safe\" half of this row is weaker than five firings suggested and must be re-stated.*\n\n*** A SECOND, UNRELATED FAILURE MODE OF THE SAME SEAT, AND IT IS ABOUT SCOPE RATHER THAN CONTENT.\nThree firings of ONE question, three scopes, measured 2026-09-08. ***\n\n    scope                                     stream     verdict?\n    regress/*.py  (a PREDICATE, ~65 files)    2,404 KB   NO -- cut short\n    six files named extensionally               325 KB   NO -- cut short\n    ONE file named                              (running)\n\n**A codex run has a budget, and a reading-scope bound spends it before the question does.**  The\nfirst scope was not a bound at all -- *\"only the probe files that contain `row(` and `exit0`\"*\ncannot be evaluated without reading every probe.  The second was properly extensional and STILL\ntoo large.\n\n> **THE RULE: a reading-scope bound must be EXTENSIONAL *and* SMALL.  Name the files, and name as\n> few as the question can be answered from.**  Where a census spans many files, ask it ONE FILE AT\n> A TIME and pick the file holding the largest share -- here `pcc2_width_probe.py`, 25 of 90\n> candidate rows.\n\n*** AND THIS IS WHY THE `codex_answer.py` VERDICT LINE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKED. ***  All three\nruns produced substantial, correct, partial work -- the membership definition, the fourth guise of\nthe class, two concrete leads.  **A reader scoring them by size or by \"did it answer\" would call all\nthree successes.**  The tool reports *\"verdict blocks 0 -- NO VERDICT LINE: treat as CUT SHORT, not\nas agreement\"*, which is the only thing separating a completed census from a budget exhaustion, and\nit is the same POSITION discriminator this project already uses for seat truncation.\n\n*Recorded under this row rather than a new one because it is the same operational question -- what\ndoes it take to get an answer out of this seat -- and this row is where a future round will look.\nThe CONTENT rule (analysis answers, construction is filtered) and the SCOPE rule (extensional and\nsmall) are independent, and a brief has to satisfy both.*",
