title: "dev_lca.c: SIX chipset registers in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set (CONF, TB_ENA, W_BASE0/1, W_MASK0/1) -- plus a seventh exit in the default arm, which this round does NOT close"
witness: "harness:ENUMERATED from the shipped source, 2026-08-31.  Eleven live terminating calls.  The value guards, each ending in exit(1): `idata \u0026 ~1` (:206), `idata \u0026 ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` (:226), `idata != 0ULL \u0026\u0026 idata != 0x300800000ULL` (:241), `idata != 0x700000ULL` (:256), `idata != 0x240000000ULL` (:280), `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` (:295).  Each diagnostic says so in its own words -- *\"(0x%\" PRIx64\") from the only implemented value\"*.  Plus two range guards that also kill: `dev \u003e= 21` (:129) and `ofs \u003e len` (:163).  NO rung-3 witness: READ, not booted."
note: "*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker\u0027s choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev \u003e= 21`, `ofs \u003e len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THE [truncated]
