VERDICT: NO-GO. The draft contains an unsupported baseline score, stale repair and scope claims, and an unfinished table.

1. **“`conf silent` read 1 on the correct build (63/64)” — UNCHECKED as a measurement.** [PRETABLE.out §8](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/PRETABLE.out:147) and the probe comment repeat this account, but I found no preserved log of that failing baseline run. `FIX518_detector_run4.out` records **64/64**, with `LCABADVAL_DONE`. Table 3 now contains **63/64 mutant results**, which do not substantiate the claimed earlier baseline result.

2. **“14/20 -> 20/20 -> 40/40”; “52/53”; “58/58”; “64/64” — these recorded scores match.** In `witness_20260909/`, both `HEAD_74daefd*.out` logs carry **14/20**; `FIX518_run1.out` carries **20/20**; `FIX518_detector_run1.out` through `run4.out` carry **40/40, 52/53, 58/58, 64/64**, respectively. Each contains `LCABADVAL_DONE`; independently counted row statuses agree. Run 2’s sole failure is `wmask0 widen`, reading zero instead of `0x100000`. The logged stages support **13, then five, then six added rows**.

3. **“Table 2 … had BASE 58/58 and M1..M24 all killed but M20_def_echo” — supported for that completed subset.** [PROGRESS.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/t2/PROGRESS.txt) agrees with its 25 corresponding raw logs; all have completion sentinels. I also checked every score in table 1’s `SCORES.txt` and `SCORES_B.txt` against its raw log: **no score mismatches**. `M29_def_allones.raw` specifically records **57/58**, failing `def read0`, with its sentinel. However, `t2/PREDICTIONS.txt` is explicitly predictive: its M7 **47/58** and M22 **15/58** differ from measured **48/58** and **16/58**. Those predictions must not become measured scores.

4. **“The main loop then ran TABLE 3 … BASE, M20, the twenty-three table-2 mutants … and table 1’s six sweepers” — completion is unsupported.** At **2026-09-10 08:57:56 UTC**, [SCORES3.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/t3/SCORES3.txt) contained only BASE **64/64**, M20 **64/64**, M25 **62/64**, and M26–M28 **63/64 each**. Their raw logs contain sentinels. **M29–M47 have no completed score rows**; M29’s raw log exists without a sentinel. The six table-1 sweepers—M26, M27, M32, M33, M35 and M37—also have no table-3 score rows. That is **six completed runs of 31 planned**, including BASE. No closing pass or closing denominator is present.

5. **“What they had measured stood, every log carrying its sentinel” — requires an explicit exclusion.** [README.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/README.txt) says the first table attempt was invalidated when its source baseline changed mid-run. Its BASE **40/40**, M1 **36/40**, and M2 **38/40** were quarantined, not retained as valid table results. `pre_repair_aborted/M3_base0_kill.PARTIAL.raw` lacks `LCABADVAL_DONE`. The completed replacement runs have sentinels; “every log” does not.

6. **“Kills measured on the 58-row instrument stand … because added rows can only add reds” — the stated justification is unsupported.** Comparing the [58-row frozen probe](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/lca_badval_probe.REPAIRED58.asrun.py) with the table-3 copy shows changes to an existing session, including the prior value and operations feeding `wmask0 hif`. This was not solely appending independent assertions. Historical 58-row measurements remain valid historical results; automatic preservation under the changed instrument needs a separate argument or measurement.

7. **“Table 1’s own additions swept … four ways and each was a vacuous row” — conflates mutants, gaps and rows.** [SCORES.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/SCORES.txt) and [SCORES_B.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/SCORES_B.txt) contain **six nonequivalent 58/58 sweepers**: M26, M27, M32, M33, M35 and M37, plus equivalent M20. PRETABLE groups them into four coverage problems. Missing silence assertions were absent rows, not existing vacuous rows. Table 1 contains **36 distinct measured mutants**, numbered M1–M35 and M37; there is no M36 result.

8. **The manual claims generally check; the path and access-line reference need precision.** I opened every requested passage in the actual [manual](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9586), which is under **`GXEMUL/_scratchpad/`**, one directory above SEC. Table 6-6 contains the thirteen stated encodings; §6.4.14 requires zero at both bases; Table 6-13 and the legend support ignoring TB_ENA’s reserved bits; Table 6-10 describes the two-bit CFG field; Figure 6-9 agrees with the header. For **“S6.4 (`:10175`) makes the accesses quadword,”** line 10175 is the heading; the actual requirement is at **10180–10181**. The two-bit field does not establish four valid PCI cycle types.

9. **“Eight transports + the Opus seat + the main loop” — does not describe eight completed transport reviews.** [SEATS.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/pass2_20260910/SEATS.txt) records **six answers from seven attempted scriptable transports**, with agy deliberately unfired. Codex’s **NO-GO**, Grok’s **NOT RATIFY**, DeepSeek’s **NOT RATIFY**, Kimi’s weekly-limit **403**, and substitute coverage are supported. “GLM / minimax / kimi-substitute conditional” loses material distinctions: GLM says the floor is incomplete; Minimax says “PASS WITH ONE UNCAUGHT CELL”; Kimi identifies a live detector defect. None is a saved clearance of the final 64-row instrument.

10. **“All were repaired … in two rounds” — contradicted by the repair record and current probe.** [PRETABLE §8](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/PRETABLE.out:147) explicitly adds round three. Furthermore, Codex’s saved answer identifies cases still absent from the current probe: legal zero at both W_MASK registers, the extra accepted encoding at MASK1, and BASE1-specific field coverage. Table 2’s M43/M44/M45/M47 predictions describe additional sibling gaps whose table-3 measurements remain outstanding. There is no artifact supporting “all repaired.”

11. **“The SG-drop cell … was never a hole” — needs an arm qualification.** `wbase0 live` does cover SG removal at BASE0, including a mutation applied to both bases. It does not cover removal at BASE1 alone; [PREDICTIONS.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/t2/PREDICTIONS.txt) explicitly names that as M45. Also, the current probe’s D6 comment still repeats the rejected claim that no row drove bit 32. The stated correction has not reached all records.

12. **“Bit 1 (type-1 cycles, not modelled)” and “Reworded” — the earlier description remains wrong, and the code correction is incomplete.** [dev_lca.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:55) still defines `LCA_W_IOC_CONF_CFG1` with the comment “type-1 cycles not modelled.” The consumer at line 195 selects type 1 using bit **0**. The revised arm and diagnostic correctly distinguish CFG encodings 10/11, but neither that revision nor the later draft paragraph corrects the stale opening description or definition comment.

13. **“Option-set member 3 throughout” and the attribution to the “flagship ruling 2026-09-09” — contradicted by the named ruling.** The `lcabadval` entries in [ledger.json](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json) describe **four window arms**, assign CONF to **member 4, ACCEPT**, and assess TB_ENA outside the round, disclosing its ignored-bit problem. The September 8 design likewise prescribes accepting and storing normalized fields, with decline for unsupported masks and default writes. The eventual six-arm implementation needs its scope revision recorded; the September 9 ruling does not establish “member 3 throughout.”

14. **“The default arm declines a write with no state change” — literally false.** [The default arm](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:469) changes `d->warned` on the first announcement in each direction. “No IOC register-value change” describes the implementation more accurately. Separately, the probe does not establish named-register preservation across default writes—the missing observation Codex identified.

15. **“Every `stored` session writes the accepted value FIRST so a skipped store reads the accepted value, not zero” — overstates what that construction guarantees.** In [the probe](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_badval_probe.py), TB_ENA’s accepted and normalized legal values are both `0x80`; declining the later write leaves that row green. Deleting an assignment also prevents the preliminary accepted store. Accepted-first protects against particular later-write declines, not every skipped store. The added `tbena clear` row addresses one such distinction; the original prose remains stale.

16. **“`wmask{0,1} declined` / `once` / `kept2` / `indep`” in the 40-row description — implies a symmetry the named log lacks.** `FIX518_detector_run1.out` has five mask-detector rows: `wmask0 declined`, `wmask0 once`, `wmask0 kept2`, `wmask1 declined`, and `wmask1 indep`. `wmask1 once` first appears in run 2. There is no corresponding full eight-row family. The heading **“shipped … 40 rows”** also needs to identify this as the initial instrument: the current untracked probe has 64 rows.

17. **“The PRE-TABLE CHECK was written before table 1 … mtime is the proof” — the present mtime proves no such ordering.** PRETABLE’s modification time is **08:28:00 UTC**, after table 1 began; the aborted BASE log was created at **05:05:10 UTC**. PRETABLE’s creation time is earlier, and its first 97 lines match the preserved `brief_sent.md` copy, supporting preservation of that initial text. Those facts do not make the current mtime proof of the asserted chronology.

18. **“Claim-to-row for every added comment” and “once per device instance is untestable … (codex)” — overstate the saved review.** Codex says per-instance behavior is **untested**, and that PRETABLE lacks its disclosure. Neither PRETABLE’s appendices nor the current probe supplies that explicit limitation. “Untestable” is not what the seat established. The same answer identifies the missing observation of register state across default writes.

19. **“The `-q` clause is … not this diff’s claim” — directly contradicted by the diff.** [The newly added helper comment](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:305) expressly claims that `fatal()` cannot be silenced by `-q`. `debugmsg.c` supports the behavior by inspection, but the supplied probe does not run with `-q`; the saved Codex answer distinguishes source support from missing test coverage. Calling it someone else’s fact does not remove it from the added claims.

20. **“All were repaired” also overclaims the diagnostic-width repair.** [The current `def width` assertion](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_badval_probe.py:475) searches for the wide value anywhere in the complete transcript. That value is also supplied in the debugger setup command; the assertion does not require it to appear in the diagnostic. No preserved diagnostic-narrowing mutant result establishes that this repaired assertion detects truncated output.

21. **“§4 had named four mutants that §5’s list dropped … restored” — not fully supported by the actual mutators.** PRETABLE §4 names `M_wen_perreg`, specifically **two WEN latch bits**. Neither table’s mutator implements that change; disabling one arm’s announcement is a different mutation. The originally described narrowed W_MASK extraction mask is also absent; widening and raw-store variants do not restore that exact prescribed edit. Appending different cases does not establish restoration of all four.

22. **The stale-index explanation involving “M20_def_echo … would have passed all 40” needs to preserve the distinction already in Codex’s answer.** The old index is demonstrably wrong, and Kimi identified it. But M20’s actual edit leaves default reads returning zero regardless of the index; its sweep cannot measure that assertion defect. Codex’s pass-2 Q2 already explains this equivalence. Table 1’s **57/58 M29** result supports the repaired nonzero-read discriminator.

23. **“Round three … two silence sessions” — the current construction has one silence session and one announcing session.** [D9](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_badval_probe.py:547) combines WEN-clear BASE0 and bit-1-clear CONF in the first session. The second writes CONF `0x6` and expects an announcement. The session split is present; describing both sessions as silence tests is wrong.

